Founded as a defense contractor, Palantir has since expanded to the private sector. These activities now provide a large proportion of the company's revenue. Palantir had 55% year-over-year growth in the U.S. commercial market in Q2 2024; it also serves foreign customers. Applications include telecommunications and infrastructure planning.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Q2 2024 {{!}} Letter to Shareholders |url=https://www.palantir.com/q2-2024-letter/en/ |access-date=October 7, 2024 |publisher=Palantir |language=en |archive-date=October 7, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241007161951/https://www.palantir.com/q2-2024-letter/en/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Palantir Metropolis was used by hedge funds, banks, and financial services firms.<ref name="bare_url" /><ref name="npr">{{Cite news |title=A Tech Fix For Illegal Government Snooping? |publisher=NPR |url=https://www.npr.org/templates/story/story.php?storyId=106479613 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133306/https://www.npr.org/templates/story/story.php?storyId=106479613 |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |access-date=January 30, 2012}}</ref><ref name="bare_url_a">{{Cite web |last=Chiang |first=Oliver |date=April 30, 2010 |title=PayPal-Based Technology Helped Bust India's And The Dalai Lama's Cyberspies |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/firewall/2010/04/30/paypal-based-technology-helped-bust-indias-and-the-dalai-lamas-cyberspies/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170814002848/https://www.forbes.com/sites/firewall/2010/04/30/paypal-based-technology-helped-bust-indias-and-the-dalai-lamas-cyberspies/ |archive-date=August 14, 2017 |access-date=September 7, 2017 |magazine=Forbes}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=A Human Driven Data-centric Approach to Accountability: Analyzing Data to Prevent Fraud, Waste and Abuse in Stimulus Spending: Gov 2.0 Expo 2010 - Co-produced by UBM TechWeb & O'Reilly Conferences, May 25 - 27, 2010, Washington, DC |url=http://www.gov2expo.com/gov2expo2010/public/schedule/detail/13996 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133339/https://www.oreilly.com/conferences/ |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |access-date=January 30, 2012 |publisher=Gov2expo.com}}</ref> Palantir Foundry clients include [[Merck KGaA]],<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 20, 2018 |title=Merck KGaA, Palantir's new joint venture looks to tear down cancer research data silos |url=https://www.mobihealthnews.com/content/merck-kgaa-palantirs-new-joint-venture-looks-tear-down-cancer-research-data-silos |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133339/https://www.mobihealthnews.com/content/merck-kgaa-palantirs-new-joint-venture-looks-tear-down-cancer-research-data-silos |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |access-date=February 27, 2019 |website=MobiHealthNews |language=en}}</ref> [[Airbus]],<ref>{{Cite web |date=October 23, 2018 |title=Airbus believes its AI can eliminate flight delays |url=https://venturebeat.com/2018/10/22/airbus-believes-its-ai-can-eliminate-flight-delays/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133340/https://venturebeat.com/2018/10/22/airbus-believes-its-ai-can-eliminate-flight-delays/ |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |access-date=February 27, 2019 |website=VentureBeat}}</ref> and [[Scuderia Ferrari|Ferrari]].<ref>{{Cite press release |last=Palantir |title=Palantir Foundry Enables Scuderia Ferrari Through Data |url=https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/palantir-foundry-enables-scuderia-ferrari-through-data-873451908.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133340/https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/palantir-foundry-enables-scuderia-ferrari-through-data-873451908.html |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |website=PRNewsWire |access-date=February 27, 2019}}</ref> Palantir partner [[Information Warfare Monitor]] used Palantir software to uncover both the [[Ghostnet]] and the [[Shadow Network]].<ref name="bare_url_a" /><ref>{{Cite news |title=CNN.com Video |publisher=CNN |url=http://www.cnn.com/video/#/video/tech/2009/03/30/vause.china.cyber.espionage.cnn |url-status=live |access-date=June 26, 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20000816030234/http://www.cnn.com/video/#/video/tech/2009/03/30/vause.china.cyber.espionage.cnn |archive-date=August 16, 2000}}</ref><ref name="Markoff">{{Cite news |last=Markoff |first=John |date=March 29, 2009 |title=Vast Spy System Loots Computers in 103 Countries |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2009/03/29/technology/29spy.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090401224950/http://www.nytimes.com/2009/03/29/technology/29spy.html |archive-date=April 1, 2009 |access-date=February 25, 2017 |work=The New York Times}}</ref>

===NATO===
[[NATO]] acquired the Palantir Maven Smart System NATO on March 25, 2025.<ref>{{cite web |title=NATO acquires AI-enabled Warfighting System |url=https://shape.nato.int/news-releases/nato-acquires-aienabled-warfighting-system- |access-date=3 July 2026 |date=14 April 2025}}</ref> The system reached full technical operational capability on June 22, 2026. The initial report issued by NATO on June 30, 2026, said that it is compatible with any AI system such as the U.S.' Meta Platforms and France's Mistral AI, with integration of other systems planned.<ref>{{cite web |title=NATO Maven Smart System Achieves Full Technical Operational Capability |url=https://shape.nato.int/news-releases/Maven-System-achieves-full-capability |website=shape.nato.int |language=en |date=30 June 2026}}</ref> NATO commanders have reacted to the discussion on sovereignty in the EU member countries. In May 2026, Admiral [[Pierre Vandier]], [[Allied Command Transformation|NATO's supreme allied commander transformation]], said that currently there is no competitor for Palantir, and if European countries want alternatives, these alternatives should prove themselves in a few months or years, and not a decade. He advised countries to focus on control over processed data in the short term and development of infrastructure (like cloud and chips) in the long term, and not to mix these problems with dependence on single suppliers, which can be dealt with by pushing suppliers toward interoperable systems.<ref>{{cite news| title=NATO commander: Europe has no alternative to Palantir's warfare tech for now |url=https://www.politico.eu/article/nato-commander-europe-no-palantir-alternative/ |work=POLITICO |date=20 May 2026}}</ref> {{ill|Kai Rohrschneider|de}}, commander of [[Allied Joint Support and Enabling Command]], criticized the distrust towards the United States and praised the Palantir software's "enormous value", noting that if member countries want to use different systems on the national level, connectivity between such systems and the programs of the NATO commands must be established.<ref>{{cite news |title=Bundeswehr will mächtiges KI-Spionage-Tool aus USA nicht nutzen – doch NATO-Kommandeur schwärmt |url=https://www.merkur.de/politik/palantir-deutscher-nato-kommandeur-lobt-umstrittene-us-software-94309561.html |work=www.merkur.de |date=17 May 2026 |language=de}}</ref>

===U.S. civil entities===
Palantir's software was used by the [[Recovery Accountability and Transparency Board]] to detect and investigate fraud and abuse in the [[American Recovery and Reinvestment Act]]. Specifically, the Recovery Operations Center (ROC) used Palantir to integrate transactional data with open-source and private data sets to better screen the entities receiving stimulus funds.<ref name="bare_url_b" /> Other clients as of 2019 included [[Polaris Project]],<ref>{{Cite web |last=Knox |first=Karin |date=January 24, 2014 |title=How Many Years A Slave? |url=https://www.palantir.com/2014/01/how-many-years-a-slave/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304125053/https://www.palantir.com/2014/01/how-many-years-a-slave/ |archive-date=March 4, 2016}}</ref> the [[Centers for Disease Control and Prevention]], the [[National Center for Missing and Exploited Children]],<ref name="tebtecnchrun" /> the [[National Institutes of Health]],<ref>{{Cite web |date=September 24, 2018 |title=With $7M contract, NIH taps big data analysis platform Palantir to streamline health research |url=https://www.mobihealthnews.com/content/7m-contract-nih-taps-big-data-analysis-platform-palantir-streamline-health-research |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133340/https://www.mobihealthnews.com/content/7m-contract-nih-taps-big-data-analysis-platform-palantir-streamline-health-research |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |access-date=February 28, 2019 |website=MobiHealthNews |language=en}}</ref> [[Team Rubicon]],<ref>{{Cite news |title=Can Data-Crunching Palantir Find Missing Children and Catch Traffickers? |url=http://fortune.com/2016/03/14/how-palantir-uses-big-data-to-find-missing-kids/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133342/https://fortune.com/2016/03/14/how-palantir-uses-big-data-to-find-missing-kids/ |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |access-date=February 28, 2019 |website=Fortune}}</ref> and the United Nations [[World Food Programme]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=February 5, 2019 |title=Palantir and WFP partner to help transform global humanitarian delivery |publisher=World Food Programme |url=https://www1.wfp.org/news/palantir-and-wfp-partner-help-transform-global-humanitarian-delivery |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133344/https://www.wfp.org/news/palantir-and-wfp-partner-help-transform-global-humanitarian-delivery |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |access-date=February 28, 2019}}</ref> In October 2020, Palantir began helping the federal government set up a system to track the manufacture, distribution, and administration of [[Coronavirus disease 2019|COVID-19]] vaccines across the country.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Winkler |first=Peter Loftus and Rolfe |date=October 22, 2020 |title=Palantir to Help U.S. Track Covid-19 Vaccines |language=en-US |work=[[The Wall Street Journal]] |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/palantir-to-help-u-s-track-covid-19-vaccines-11603367276 |url-status=live |access-date=October 23, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133343/https://trc.taboola.com/advance-pennlivecom/log/3/visible?tvi2=2512&route=LA%3ALA%3AV&lti=deflated |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |issn=0099-9660}}</ref> From November 2023 to March 2026, [[NYC Health + Hospitals]] contracted with Palantir to increase revenue collection.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Biddle |first=Sam |date=2026-02-15 |title=Palantir Gets Millions of Dollars From New York City's Public Hospitals |url=https://theintercept.com/2026/02/15/palantir-contract-new-york-city-health-hospitals/ |access-date=2026-04-21 |website=The Intercept |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Mansoor |first=Sanya |date=2026-03-26 |title=New York City hospitals drop Palantir as controversial AI firm expands in UK |url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/mar/26/new-york-hospitals-palantir-ai |access-date=2026-04-21 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> In May 2026, the [[United States Department of Agriculture]] entered into a $3.9 million contract with Palantir to surveil employees to enforce its [[Return to office|return-to-office]] policy.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Curry Wimbish |first=Whitney |date=2026-05-18 |title=Palantir Gets an Initial $3.9 Million to Spy on Federal Workers |url=https://prospect.org/2026/05/18/palantir-federal-workers-surveillance-usda-social-security-veterans-affairs/ |access-date=2026-05-25 |website=The American Prospect |language=en-US}}</ref>

===U.S. military, intelligence, and police===
Palantir Gotham is used by counter-terrorism analysts at offices in the [[United States Intelligence Community]] and [[United States Department of Defense]], fraud investigators at the [[Recovery Accountability and Transparency Board]], and cyber analysts at [[Information Warfare Monitor]] (responsible for the [[GhostNet]] and the [[Shadow Network]] investigation). Gotham was used by fraud investigators at the [[Recovery Accountability and Transparency Board]], a former U.S. federal agency which operated from 2009 to 2015. Other clients as of 2013 included [[DHS]], [[NSA]], [[FBI]], the [[U.S. Marine Corps]], the [[U.S. Air Force]], [[U.S. Special Operations Command]], [[West Point]], the [[Joint IED Defeat Organization]], and Allies. The [[U.S. Army]] continued to use its own data analysis tool.<ref name="tebtecnchrun" /> According to [[TechCrunch]], "The U.S. spy agencies also employed Palantir to connect databases across departments. Before this, most of the databases used by the CIA and FBI were siloed, forcing users to search each database individually. Now everything is linked together using Palantir."<ref name="tebtecnchrun" />

U.S. [[military intelligence]] used the Palantir product to improve its ability to predict locations of [[improvised explosive devices]] in its [[war in Afghanistan (2001–present)|war in Afghanistan]]. Field practitioners in Afghanistan reported that Palantir's analytical capabilities provided better support than the U.S. Army's [[Program of Record]], the [[Distributed Common Ground System]] (DCGS-A), though adoption remained limited across the theater. Congressman [[Duncan D. Hunter]] complained of [[United States Department of Defense]] obstacles to its wider use in 2012.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Scarborough |first=Rowan |author-link=Rowan Scarborough |date=July 16, 2012 |title=Military has to fight to purchase lauded IED buster |work=[[The Washington Times]] |url=http://p.washingtontimes.com/news/2012/jul/16/military-has-to-fight-to-purchase-lauded-ied-buste/ |url-status=live |access-date=September 29, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120719162415/http://p.washingtontimes.com/news/2012/jul/16/military-has-to-fight-to-purchase-lauded-ied-buste/ |archive-date=July 19, 2012}}</ref> Palantir has also been reported to be working with various U.S. police departments, for example accepting a contract in 2013 to support the Northern California Regional Intelligence Center in developing a [[license plate]] reader database. Civil liberties organizations raised concerns about potential mass surveillance and privacy implications.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Blagdon |first=Jeff |date=June 29, 2013 |title=Palantir is helping California police develop controversial license plate database |url=https://www.theverge.com/2013/6/29/4478748/california-license-plate-reader-database-palantir |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133350/https://www.theverge.com/2013/6/29/4478748/california-license-plate-reader-database-palantir |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |access-date=September 7, 2017 |website=[[The Verge]]}}</ref> In 2012, the [[New Orleans Police Department]] partnered with Palantir to create a predictive policing program.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Winston |first=Ali |date=February 27, 2018 |title=Palantir Has Secretly Been Using New Orleans to Test its Predictive Policing Technology |url=https://www.theverge.com/2018/2/27/17054740/palantir-predictive-policing-tool-new-orleans-nopd |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180228150754/https://www.theverge.com/2018/2/27/17054740/palantir-predictive-policing-tool-new-orleans-nopd |archive-date=February 28, 2018 |access-date=February 28, 2018 |website=[[The Verge]]}}</ref>

In 2014, [[United States Immigration and Customs Enforcement|U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement]] (ICE) awarded Palantir a $41 million contract to build and maintain a new intelligence system called Investigative Case Management (ICM) to track personal and criminal records of legal and [[illegal immigrant]]s. This application was originally conceived by ICE's office of Homeland Security Investigations (HSI), allowing its users access to other federal and private law enforcement entities' intelligence platforms. The system reached its "final operation capacity" under the Trump administration in September 2017.<ref name="DeportationMachine" /> In 2021, ICE announced that it would terminate its contract with Palantir as it was developing its own technology. But a year later, as replacement proved unsuccessful, ICE reversed that decision.{{sfn|Steinberger|2025|p=227}}

Palantir took over the Pentagon's [[Project Maven]] contract in 2019 after Google decided not to continue developing AI unmanned drones used for bombings and intelligence.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=December 2019 |title=Palantir took over from Google on Project Maven |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/palantir-took-over-from-google-on-project-maven-2019-12 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133358/https://www.businessinsider.com/palantir-took-over-from-google-on-project-maven-2019-12 |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |access-date=January 17, 2020 |website=Business Insider}}</ref> In 2024, Palantir emerged as a "Trump trade" for further enforcing the law on illegal immigrants and profiting on federal spending for national security and immigration.<ref>[https://www.forbes.com/sites/greatspeculations/2024/11/21/palantir-stock-at-63-the-good-bad--ugly/ "Palantir Stock At $63: The Good, Bad And Ugly"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241226030254/https://www.forbes.com/sites/greatspeculations/2024/11/21/palantir-stock-at-63-the-good-bad--ugly/ |date=December 26, 2024}} ''Forbes''. Retrieved December 17, 2024.</ref> Palantir has a $30 million contract with ICE to track the movement of migrants. The [[Department of Government Efficiency]] asked Palantir to help it speed up deportation by creating a master database.<ref>[https://www.npr.org/2025/05/01/nx-s1-5372776/palantir-tech-contracts-trump "How Palantir, the secretive tech company, is rising in the Trump era"] NPR. Retrieved June 21, 2025.</ref> On July 31, 2025, the U.S. Army awarded Palantir an "Enterprise Service Agreement" valued at up to $10 billion over 10 years. This contract vehicle consolidates 75 previously separate data and software contracts (in 15 of which Palantir is the prime contractor and in 60 of which it is a subcontractor) into a single software contract, streamlining the Army's procurement of Palantir's AI, data integration, and analytics tools.<ref>{{cite web |title=U.S. Army Awards Enterprise Service Agreement to Enhance Military Readiness and Drive Operational Efficiency |url=https://www.army.mil/article/287506/u_s_army_awards_enterprise_service_agreement_to_enhance_military_readiness_and_drive_operational_efficiency |publisher=United States Army |date=July 31, 2025 |access-date=April 14, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Army consolidates dozens of Palantir software contracts into one deal worth up to $10 billion |url=https://breakingdefense.com/2025/08/army-consolidates-dozens-of-palantir-software-contracts-into-one-deal-worth-up-to-10-billion/ |work=Breaking Defense |date=August 1, 2025 |access-date=April 14, 2026}}</ref>

=== Financial Conduct Authority (United Kingdom) ===
In March 2026, the [[Financial Conduct Authority]] (FCA), the [[United Kingdom]]'s primary financial regulatory body, awarded Palantir a contract to investigate its highly sensitive regulation data. ''The Guardian'' reported that Palantir beat one unnamed competitor to win the contract. The deal has raised serious concerns inside and outside the FCA, as it gives Palantir access to data including whole email accounts, phone calls, aggregated social media posts, and full financial records.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Booth |first=Robert |date=2026-03-22 |title=Palantir extends reach into British state as it gets access to sensitive FCA data |url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/mar/22/palantir-extends-reach-into-british-state-as-it-gets-access-to-sensitive-fca-data |access-date=2026-03-23 |work=[[The Guardian]] |language=en |issn=0261-3077}}</ref>

=== NHS England (United Kingdom) ===
Palantir has contracts relating to patient data from England's [[National Health Service (England)|National Health Service]]. In 2020, it was awarded an emergency non-competitive contract to mine COVID-19 patient data and consolidate government databases to help ministers and officials respond to the pandemic. The contract was valued at more than £23.5 million and extended for two more years. The awarding of the contract without competition was heavily criticised, prompting the NHS to pledge an open and transparent procurement process for any future data contract.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Lewis |first1=Paul |last2=Conn |first2=David |last3=Pegg |first3=David |title=UK government using confidential patient data in coronavirus response |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2020/apr/12/uk-government-using-confidential-patient-data-in-coronavirus-response |website=The Guardian |access-date=January 22, 2024 |date=April 12, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Times |first1=Byline |last2=Hencke |first2=David |date=April 22, 2020 |title=Palantir Coronavirus Contract Did Not Go to Competitive Tender |url=https://bylinetimes.com/2020/04/22/palantir-coronavirus-contract-did-not-go-to-competitive-tender/ |access-date=April 14, 2023 |website=Byline Times |language=en-GB |archive-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201005142910/https://bylinetimes.com/2020/04/22/palantir-coronavirus-contract-did-not-go-to-competitive-tender// |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Commentary: We've won our lawsuit over UK govt's £23m NHS data deal with Palantir |url=https://www.business-humanrights.org/en/latest-news/commentary-weve-won-our-lawsuit-over-uk-23m-nhs-data-deal-with-palantir/ |access-date=April 14, 2023 |publisher=Business & Human Rights Resource Centre |language=en |archive-date=April 14, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230414151009/https://www.business-humanrights.org/en/latest-news/commentary-weve-won-our-lawsuit-over-uk-23m-nhs-data-deal-with-palantir/ |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Liam Fox]] encouraged the firm "to expand their software business" in Britain.<ref>{{cite news |date=June 29, 2021 |title=UK trade department courted US firm Palantir over 'untapped' NHS data potential |work=Politico |url=https://www.politico.eu/article/uk-trade-department-courted-us-firm-palantir-over-untapped-nhs-data-potential/ |access-date=July 6, 2021}}</ref> It was said to be "critical to the success of the vaccination and PPE programmes", but its involvement with the NHS was controversial among civil liberties groups.<ref>{{cite news |date=June 9, 2022 |title=Palantir gears up to expand its reach into UK's NHS |newspaper=Financial Times |url=https://www.ft.com/content/3f6f24f8-9e5c-42c3-8ae6-bfef5f953524 |access-date=June 9, 2022 |archive-date=June 9, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220609041059/https://www.ft.com/content/3f6f24f8-9e5c-42c3-8ae6-bfef5f953524 |url-status=live}}</ref> Conservative MP [[David Davis (British politician)|David Davis]] called for a judicial review into the sharing of patient data with Palantir.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Clark |first=Lindsay |title=Backbench Tory campaigner promises judicial review of data grab of English GP patients unless UK government changes tack |url=https://www.theregister.com/2021/06/25/david_davis_gpdpr/ |access-date=April 14, 2023 |website=The Register}}</ref>

The procurement of a £480m [[Federated database system|Federated Data Platform]] by [[NHS England]], launched in January 2023, has been called a "must win" for Palantir.<ref>{{Cite news |date=June 9, 2022 |title=Palantir gears up to expand its reach into UK's NHS |work=Financial Times |url=https://www.ft.com/content/3f6f24f8-9e5c-42c3-8ae6-bfef5f953524 |access-date=March 31, 2023}}</ref> The procurement has been called a "farce" by civil liberties campaigners who allege that Palantir has a competitive advantage as it "already has its feet under the table in NHS England" and benefits from a short procurement window.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Glaze |first=Ben |date=February 7, 2023 |title=Campaigners warning over US tech giant's NHS links as deadline for deal looms |url=https://www.mirror.co.uk/news/politics/campaigners-warning-over-tech-giants-29154470 |access-date=April 4, 2023 |website=The Daily Mirror |language=en |archive-date=April 4, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404124131/https://www.mirror.co.uk/news/politics/campaigners-warning-over-tech-giants-29154470 |url-status=live}}</ref> In April 2023 it was revealed that a consortium of UK companies had unsuccessfully bid for the contract.<ref>{{Cite web |last=says |first=Basil_Bekdash |date=April 13, 2023 |title=UK Consortium unsuccessful in its bid for £480M Federated Data Platform |url=https://www.digitalhealth.net/2023/04/uk-consortium-unsuccessful-in-its-bid-for-480m-federated-data-platform/ |access-date=April 14, 2023 |website=Digital Health |language=en-GB}}</ref> In April 2023, Davis publicly expressed his concern over the procurement process, saying it could become a "battle royale". He was one of a dozen MPs pressing the government over privacy concerns with the use of data. Labour peer and former Health Minister [[Philip Hunt, Baron Hunt of Kings Heath|Philip Hunt]] voiced his concern about Palantir's use of data, saying, "The current NHS and current government doesn't have a good track record of getting the details right, and the procurement shows no sign of going better."<ref name="Mason">{{Cite news |last1=Mason |first2=Rowena |last2=Whitehall |date=April 27, 2023 |title=Ex-minister predicts 'battle royale' over US firm's bid for NHS data contract |language=en-GB |work=The Guardian |url=https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2023/apr/27/ex-minister-predicts-battle-royale-over-us-firms-bid-for-nhs-data-contract |access-date=April 28, 2023 |issn=0261-3077}}</ref>

Also in April 2023, ''The Guardian'' reported that 11 NHS trusts had suspended use of Palantir Foundry software pending resolution of technical implementation issues, according to a Department of Health and Social Care spokesperson.<ref name="Mason" /> In March 2023, it was revealed that NHS hospitals had been ordered to share patient data with Palantir, prompting renewed criticism from civil liberties groups, including for supporting [[Gaza genocide|genocide]], privacy and security practices, and "buying their way in".<ref name=":4" /><ref>{{Cite news |last2=Amin |first1=Ali | last1=Mitib | first2=Lucas |title=NHS tells hospitals to share medical records with US spy-tech company |newspaper=[[The Times]] |language=en |url=https://www.thetimes.com/uk/healthcare/article/nhs-tells-hospitals-to-share-medical-records-with-us-spy-tech-company-qhs3397ft |access-date=March 31, 2023 |issn=0140-0460 |archive-date=March 31, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230331153018/https://www.thetimes.co.uk/article/nhs-tells-hospitals-to-share-medical-records-with-us-spy-tech-company-qhs3397ft |url-status=live}}</ref> Groups including the [[The Doctors' Association UK|Doctors' Association UK]], [[National Pensioners Convention|National Pensioners' Convention]], and Just Treatment subsequently threatened legal action over NHS England's procurement of the FDP contract, citing concerns over the use of patient data.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Clark |first=Lindsay |title=NHS threatened with legal action over patient data platform |url=https://www.theregister.com/2023/03/17/nhs_threatened_with_legal_action/ |access-date=March 31, 2023 |website=The Register |archive-date=March 30, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230330114327/https://www.theregister.com/2023/03/17/nhs_threatened_with_legal_action/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

In 2022, Palantir recruited [[NHS England]]'s former artificial intelligence chief, Indra Joshi. It said it was planning to increase its team in the UK by 250.<ref>{{cite news |date=April 22, 2022 |title=NHS's AI chief joins CIA-linked US tech firm |website=Health Service Journal |url=https://www.hsj.co.uk/technology-and-innovation/nhss-ai-chief-joins-cia-linked-us-tech-firm/7032331.article |access-date=May 24, 2022}}</ref> Palantir's UK head, [[Louis Mosley]], grandson of the former [[British Union of Fascists]] leader [[Oswald Mosley]],<ref>{{cite news |last=Brown|first=Larisa|date=September 22, 2024|title=How tech giant Palantir is secretly changing the face of warfare|url=https://www.thetimes.com/uk/crime/article/military-technology-database-palantir-royal-navy-war-fn0gb9rjb|location= |work=[[The Times]]|access-date=April 17, 2025}}</ref> was quoted internally as saying that Palantir's strategy for entry into the British health industry was to "Buy our way in" by acquiring smaller rival companies with existing relationships with the NHS to "take a lot of ground and take down a lot of political resistance".<ref>{{Cite news |date=September 30, 2022 |title=Peter Thiel's Palantir Had Secret Plan to Crack UK's NHS: 'Buying Our Way In' |language=en |work=Bloomberg |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-09-30/palantir-had-plan-to-crack-uk-health-system-buying-our-way-in |access-date=April 4, 2023 |archive-date=January 24, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230124224007/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-09-30/palantir-had-plan-to-crack-uk-health-system-buying-our-way-in |url-status=live}}</ref> In November 2023, NHS England awarded Palantir a £330 million contract to create and manage the Federated Data Platform.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Trickett |first1=Jon |title=NHS data given to CIA-backed Palantir |url=https://morningstaronline.co.uk/article/f/hold-nhs-data-given-cia-backed-palantir |website=Morning Star |access-date=December 2, 2023 |language=en |date=December 2, 2023 |archive-date=December 2, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231202071727/https://morningstaronline.co.uk/article/f/hold-nhs-data-given-cia-backed-palantir |url-status=live}}</ref> In April 2024, medical professionals picketed at NHS England headquarters, demanding that it end its contract with Palantir over contracts with the [[Israel Defense Forces|IDF]].<ref name="Telegraph April 2024" />
=== Ministry of Defence (United Kingdom) ===

Palantir has held contracts with the United Kingdom Ministry of Defence (MoD) relating to data analytics capabilities supporting strategic, tactical, and live decision-making.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.ft.com/content/5bba355e-b8e3-4bc3-b440-750a23f8d48c |title=UK defence data contract coverage |work=Financial Times |access-date=12 February 2026}}</ref>

In December 2022, Palantir was awarded a £75 million Enterprise Agreement to provide its software toolset to meet the UK MoD's requirements across all security classifications for a three-year period, aligned with defence data strategies.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.contractsfinder.service.gov.uk/notice/ac82a048-4e00-46f0-8a8f-be81beeddcca |title=Enterprise Agreement for provision of Palantir software tool-set |website=Contracts Finder |access-date=12 February 2026}}</ref>

In December 2025, the Ministry of Defence awarded Palantir a further £240 million contract to support data analytics capabilities enabling critical strategic, tactical, and live decision-making across defence operations, including interoperability with NATO systems.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.find-tender.service.gov.uk/Notice/006088-2026 |title=Contract award notice: Data analytics support services |website=Find a Tender Service |access-date=12 February 2026}}</ref> This contract was a direct award, meaning no other companies were considered during the procurement process.<ref name=":11">{{cite news |last1=Langford |first1=Craig |title=MOD confirms Palantir contract was direct award |url=https://ukdefencejournal.org.uk/mod-confirms-palantir-contract-was-direct-award/ |date=18 February 2026}}</ref> The reasons given for this exception were that switching supplier (Palantir were awarded a previous contract) would cause technical difficulties, and that no other supplier could provide a similar service.<ref name=":11" /> 

The 2025 contract attracted political scrutiny, with critics raising concerns about transparency surrounding meetings between UK political figures and representatives of the company.<ref><!-- Add high-quality reliable source here --></ref>

=== Metropolitan police (United Kingdom) ===
In February 2026, it was revealed that Scotland Yard was using AI tools supplied by Palantir to profile its officers, specifically to look at their illness levels, absences from duty and overtime patterns.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Booth |first=Robert |date=2026-02-22 |title=Met police using AI tools supplied by Palantir to flag officer misconduct |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2026/feb/22/met-police-ai-tools-officer-misconduct-palantir |access-date=2026-03-18 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> Responding to FOI requests, the Metropolitan police refused to confirm or deny whether it had used Palantir's technology since 2021 but confirmed it had not used any between November 2019 and July 2021.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-02-28 |title=UK police forces dodge questions on Palantir |url=https://goodlawproject.org/uk-police-forces-dodge-questions-on-palantir/ |access-date=2026-03-18 |publisher=Good Law Project |language=en-GB}}</ref> Palantir is included as one of 29 possible suppliers for the Metropolitan police and the Mayor's Office for Policing and Crime between 3 December 2025 and 2 December 2030 as part of its "Precise Policing 2" dynamic market.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Precise Policing 2 - Find a Tender |url=https://www.find-tender.service.gov.uk/Notice/079524-2025 |access-date=2026-03-18 |website=find-tender.service.gov.uk}}</ref>

In May 2026, [[Mayor of London]] [[Sadiq Khan]] blocked a contract between Palantir and the [[Metropolitan Police]] worth up to £50 million.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kundaliya |first=Dev |title=Sadiq Khan blocks Met's £50m AI deal with Palantir |url=https://www.computing.co.uk/news/2026/government/sadiq-khan-blocks-met-s-50m-ai-deal-with-palantir |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260528063352/https://www.computing.co.uk/news/2026/government/sadiq-khan-blocks-met-s-50m-ai-deal-with-palantir |archive-date=2026-05-28 |access-date=2026-06-03 |website=www.computing.co.uk}}</ref> The Mayor's Office for Policing and Crime (MOPAC) cited concerns with Palantir's ethics and that the Metropolitan Police had failed to demonstrate sufficient value for money.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-05-21 |title=Met Police Palantir contract blocked by City Hall |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/clyp1e11px0o |access-date=2026-06-03 |website=BBC News |language=en-GB}}</ref>

=== Territorial Police Forces (United Kingdom) ===
In November 2025, Bedfordshire police used Palantir tools to convict a criminal gang named “Fuck the Police”, which was suspected of stealing £800,000 from cash points in more than 3,000 withdrawals across the UK. Bedfordshire police seized the suspects' smartphones and used Palantir's Nectar to read and translate over 100,000 text messages from Romanian to English. Nectar analyzed their movements, combining images and text to suggest possible crimes, and created charts of the connections among those investigated.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Booth |first=Robert |date=2026-02-24 |title=‘It’s not Robocop’: UK police embrace AI ‘efficiency’ in complex investigations |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2026/feb/24/its-not-robocop-uk-police-embrace-ai-efficiency-in-complex-investigations |access-date=2026-03-18 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> 

===Europol===
[[Europol]] has used the Gotham system.<ref name="AP Pushback" />

===Denmark===
The Danish POL-INTEL predictive policing project has been operational since 2017 and is based on the Gotham system. According to the AP, the Danish system "uses a mapping system to build a so-called heat map identifying areas with higher crime rates."<ref name="AP Pushback" /> In 2025, when the Danish government was integrating Palantir into the country's military, police and intelligence services, a major argument made by opponents was that the Praxis project (backed by Thiel through Pronomos) threatened Greenland. Other arguments were concerned with Thiel's politics in the U.S., Palantir's association with U.S. and other European intelligence services (Jacob Kaarsbo, a former chief analyst at [[Danish Defence Intelligence Service]], could not name these intelligence services for reasons of confidentiality), and the security risk posed by giving Palantir citizens' data. The [[Police of Denmark|Danish National Police]] answered with a reference to a 2021 response to the [[Folketing]], but otherwise, the Police, the [[Danish Security and Intelligence Service]], and Minister of Justice Peter Hummelgaard refused to comment on the matter.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gjerding |first1=Sebastian |last2=Andersen |first2=Lasse Skou |title=Kontroversiel amerikansk virksomhed har adgang til fortrolige oplysninger hos dansk politi |url=https://www.information.dk/indland/2025/04/kontroversiel-amerikansk-virksomhed-adgang-fortrolige-oplysninger-dansk-politi?kupon=eyJpYXQiOjE3NDU3MzgxMDgsInN1YiI6IjI0MzMyOjgyOTM5NiJ9.Ixj7Qyo6zti7M6syRG9NEw |access-date=August 13, 2025 |work=Information |date=April 25, 2025 |language=da}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Seidelin |first1=Matias |title=OLFI - Kontroversielt amerikansk firma på vej ind i dansk våbenindustri |url=https://olfi.dk/2025/05/21/kontroversielt-amerikansk-firma-paa-vej-ind-i-dansk-vaabenindustri/ |date=May 21, 2025 |language=da-DK}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Bech-Nielsen |first1=Peter Christian |title=Hummelgaard trods dybt kontroversielt PET-forslag om masseovervågning: Vil ikke tale om Palantir |website=Radar |url=https://radar.dk/artikel/hummelgaard-trods-dybt-kontroversielt-pet-forslag-om-masseovervaagning-vil-ikke-tale-om-palantir |access-date=August 13, 2025 |date=May 5, 2025 |language=da}}</ref> In June 2026, Denmark granted Palantir access to the police force in Greenland.<ref>{{cite news |last1= Erhvervsjournalist |first1=Charlotte Pries |title=Rigspolitiet kobler Grønlands Politi på Palantir |url= https://www.computerworld.dk/art/295887/rigspolitiet-kobler-groenlands-politi-paa-palantir |work= Computerworld |date=11 June 2026 |language=da}}</ref>

===Norway===
Norwegian Customs uses Palantir Gotham to screen passengers and vehicles for control. Known inputs are prefiled freight documents, passenger lists, the national Currency Exchange database (tracks all cross-border currency exchanges), the [[Norwegian Labour and Welfare Administration]]s employer- and employee-registry, the Norwegian stock holder registry and 30 public databases from InfoTorg. InfoTorg provides access to more than 30 databases, including the Norwegian National Citizen registry, European Business Register, the Norwegian DMV vehicle registry, various credit databases etc. These databases are supplemented by the Norwegian Customs Departments own intelligence reports, including results of previous controls. The system is also augmented by data from public sources such as social media.<ref>{{cite news |title=Norwegian Customs implement new methods to catch smugglers |url=https://www.nettavisen.no/nyheter/dine-venner-og-nettbruk-kan-avgjore-om-du-stoppes-i-tollen/s/12-95-3423409135 |access-date=December 5, 2021 |publisher=Nettavisen |date=January 24, 2018 |archive-date=December 5, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211205175149/https://www.nettavisen.no/nyheter/dine-venner-og-nettbruk-kan-avgjore-om-du-stoppes-i-tollen/s/12-95-3423409135 |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Germany===
In 2023, German interior minister [[Nancy Faeser]] stopped the federal use of Palantir.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Faeser stoppt Anti-Gangster-Software - Reul glaubt an Wahlkampf-Manöver |work=Fokus}}</ref> As of July 2025, [[German police]] departments in three of its 16 states—[[Bavaria]], [[Hesse]], and [[Nordrhein-Westfalen]]—have used Palantir for data mining. In March, [[Baden-Württemberg]] entered a $25 million contract without a legal basis to use the software.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Volk |first=Iris |title=Baden-Württemberg: Polizei will umstrittene Palantir-Software einsetzen: BW-Grüne halten dagegen |url=https://www.tagesschau.de/inland/regional/badenwuerttemberg/swr-polizei-will-umstrittene-palantir-software-einsetzen-bw-gruene-halten-dagegen-100.html |access-date=July 30, 2025 |work=Tagesschau |language=de}}</ref> In May 2026, the Bundeswehr leadership said that, for the moment, they would not give Palantir contracts on the national level, preferring a German or European company. Inspector of cyber and information space {{ill|Thomas Daum|de|Thomas Daum (Admiral)}} said that the Bundeswehr is already working with Palantir on the NATO level, where Palantir not only provides the software but operates it, and while this operating model is efficient, it is "currently inconceivable" to hand over access to the national database to the industry's side, and the Bundeswehr wants to handle the operation of the software by the military side's personnel.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Deutschland |first1=Business Insider |title=Bundeswehr verzichtet auf Palantir – wegen Sicherheitsbedenken |url=https://www.businessinsider.de/wirtschaft/bundeswehr-verzichtet-auf-palantir-wegen-sicherheitsbedenken/ |work= Business Insider |date= 8 May 2026 |language=de}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Hong |first1=Jinju |title=Germany excludes Palantir from military AI solution |url=https://www.digitaltoday.co.kr/en/view/51699/germany-excludes-palantir-from-military-ai-solution |work= DigitalToday |date= 29 April 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

===France===
In France, after the [[November 2015 Paris attacks]], the Directorate-General for Internal Security signed a contract with Palantir, which was extended in 2019.<ref name="euractiv1"/> President [[Emmanuel Macron]] said Palantir would retain its role in the French intelligence sector until the end of his term in office, but a replacement program was launched in May 2017.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Gastineau |first1=Pierre |title=France • US' Palantir to stay part of French intelligence until end of Macron presidency? - 17/04/2025 |url=https://www.intelligenceonline.com/surveillance--interception/2025/04/17/us--palantir-to-stay-part-of-french-intelligence-until-end-of--macron-presidency,110435415-art |work=Intelligence Online |date=April 17, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> In June 2026, after the US government suddenly cut off access to [[Anthropic]]'s newest models, it was announced that France's [[General Directorate for Internal Security]] would replace Palantir with ChapsVision, a local alternative, with prime minister Sébastien Lecornu saying the government was worried about strategic dependence.<ref>{{Cite news |last= Henley |first= Jon |date= 2026-06-16 |title= France to ditch Palantir's AI data tools in favour of domestic provider |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/jun/16/france-ai-data-tools-palantir-chapsvision |access-date=2026-06-20 |work= [[The Guardian]] |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=O'Brien |first1= Chris |last2= O'Reilly-Durand |first2=Helen |title=🤖 La Machine #81: France Responds To Anthropic Fable's Sovereignty Jolt⚡ |url= https://www.frenchtechjournal.com/la-machine-81-france-responds-to-anthropic-fables-sovereignty-jolt/ |work= The French Tech Journal |date=17 June 2026 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=French security service to replace Palantir with domestic firm |url=https://www.euronews.com/business/2026/06/16/dgsi-drops-palantir-for-french-firm-says-sebastien-lecornu |work=euronews |date=16 June 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

=== Ukraine ===
Karp claims to have been the first CEO of a large U.S. company to have visited Ukraine after the [[Russian invasion of Ukraine|2022 Russian invasion]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Oshin |first=Olafmihan |date=June 6, 2022 |title=Palantir CEO travels to Ukraine for Zelensky meeting |url=https://thehill.com/policy/technology/3513256-palantir-ceo-travels-to-ukraine-for-zelensky-meeting/ |access-date=October 7, 2024 |work=The Hill |archive-date=October 7, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241007161951/https://thehill.com/policy/technology/3513256-palantir-ceo-travels-to-ukraine-for-zelensky-meeting/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Ukraine uses Palantir for military purposes such as air defense, mining warfare, logistics,<ref>{{cite news |title=AI Giant Palantir Testing its 'War Operating System' Managing Ukraine's Fight Against Russia |url=https://militarywatchmagazine.com/article/ai-giant-palantir-war-operating-system-ukraine |agency=Military Watch Magazine |date=13 May 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |date=February 8, 2024 |title=How Tech Giants Turned Ukraine Into an AI War Lab |url=https://time.com/6691662/ai-ukraine-war-palantir/ |access-date=February 15, 2024 |magazine=TIME |language=en |archive-date=February 14, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240214171053/https://time.com/6691662/ai-ukraine-war-palantir/? |url-status=live}}</ref> as well as management of the wartime economy, education system, refugees, and reconstruction activities, including organization of electronic services and demining.<ref name="united24">{{cite news |title=Palantir, the Secretive Tech Giant Shaping Ukraine's War Effort |url=https://united24media.com/war-in-ukraine/palantir-the-secretive-tech-giant-shaping-ukraines-war-effort-5519 |work=UNITED24 Media |date=31 January 2025 |language=en}}</ref><ref name="IDN">{{cite news|title="Software on the Front Line": How Palantir Is Aiding Ukraine in Its War with Russia |url=https://indepthnews.net/software-on-the-front-line-how-palantir-is-aiding-ukraine-in-its-war-with-russia/ |work=IDN-InDepthNews |date=24 August 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Chapman |first1=Lizette |title=Palantir Signs on For Reconstruction Work in War-Torn Ukraine |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2023-05-25/palantir-signs-on-for-reconstruction-work-in-war-torn-ukraine |work=Bloomberg.com |date=25 May 2023 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Kushnikov |first1=Vadim |title=Palantir Inc to provide IT assistance to the Ukrainian army |url=https://militarnyi.com/en/news/palantir-inc-to-provide-it-assistance-to-the-ukrainian-army/ |work=Militarnyi |date=29 May 2023}}</ref> 

Gotham uses data from drone videos, intercepted communications, satellite imagery, and civilian tips (using apps such as the Ukrainian "E-Enemy") to produce complete battlefield pictures that help commanders choose targets, thereby shortening the [[Kill chain (military)|"kill chain"]].<ref name="IDN"/><ref>{{cite news |last=Ignatius |first=David |author-link=David Ignatius |date=December 19, 2022 |title=How the algorithm tipped the balance in Ukraine |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2022/12/19/palantir-algorithm-data-ukraine-war/ |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221219195253/https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2022/12/19/palantir-algorithm-data-ukraine-war/ |archive-date=December 19, 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref> The Meta Constellation tool is used to organize satellite swarms while PRISMA is used to coordinate drone strikes.<ref name="IDN"/><ref name="ndtv">{{cite news |last1=Upadhayay |first1=Prapti |title=What Is Palantir's PRISMA, The AI Software Ukraine Is Using To Strike Russia |url=https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/what-is-palantirs-prisma-the-ai-software-ukraine-is-using-to-strike-russia-11577108 |date=1 June 2026}}</ref> Other platforms Ukraine uses include AIP, Foundry, and a form of Maven.<ref name="IDN"/><ref name="ndtv"/><ref name="united24"/> According to a December 2022 report by ''[[The Times]]'', Palantir's AI has allowed Ukraine to increase its artillery strikes' accuracy, speed, and deadliness.<ref>{{cite news |last=Grylls |first=George |date=December 24, 2022 |title=Ukraine is outflanking Russia with ammunition from Big Tech |url=https://www.thetimes.com/article/ukraine-is-outflanking-russia-with-ammunition-from-big-tech-lxp6sv3qz |work=[[The Times]] |archive-url=https://archive.today/20221224000114/https://www.thetimes.co.uk/article/ukraine-is-outflanking-russia-with-ammunition-from-big-tech-lxp6sv3qz |archive-date=December 24, 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref> The [[Prosecutor General of Ukraine]] also plans to use Palantir's software to help document alleged [[war crimes in the Russian invasion of Ukraine|Russian war crimes]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Wright |first=Ben |date=April 22, 2023 |title=The 'all-seeing' tech giant mapping Putin's war crimes |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/business/2023/04/22/palantir-peter-thiel-putin-war-crimes/ |newspaper=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230424094353/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/business/2023/04/22/palantir-peter-thiel-putin-war-crimes/ |archive-date=April 24, 2023}}</ref> Palantir's software is used at command centers with strong internet connections, complementing Ukraine's Delta, which serves offline situations (''[[Militarnyi]]'' links Palantir to Delta's development, but the details are not specified).<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kirichenko |first1=David |title=The Heart of War: Ukraine's Key Battlefield System |url=https://cepa.org/article/the-heart-of-war-ukraines-key-battlefield-system/ |work=CEPA |date=16 March 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Kushnikov |first1=Vadim |title=Palantir Inc to provide IT assistance to the Ukrainian army |url=https://militarnyi.com/en/news/palantir-inc-to-provide-it-assistance-to-the-ukrainian-army/ |work=Militarnyi |date=29 May 2023}}</ref> Together with Ukraine's Ministry of Defense, Palantir organizes the Brave1 Dataroom, where 100 companies use real battlefield data to train more than 80 AI models.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Levytska |first1=Iryna |title=Ukraine integrating AI into warfare together with American company Palantir – video |url=https://www.pravda.com.ua/eng/news/2026/05/12/8034299/ |work=Ukrainska Pravda |date=12 May 2026 |language=en}}</ref> It is generally recognized that Palantir provides essential wartime capabilities for Ukraine; Zelensky has called the relationship between Ukraine and Palantir "mutually beneficial".<ref name="IDN"/><ref name="carnegie">{{cite news |last1=Bienvenue |first1=Emily |last2=Kelton |first2=Maryanne |last3=Rogers |first3=Zac |last4=Sullivan |first4=Michael |last5=Ford |first5=Matthew |title=Private Tech Companies, the State, and the New Character of War |url=https://carnegieendowment.org/research/2025/12/ukraine-war-tech-companies |date=1 December 2025 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Zelensky met Palantir CEO to expand AI defence cooperation |url=https://mezha.net/eng/bukvy/2b7edbac_zelensky_met_palantir/ |work=Mezha |date=12 May 2026 |language=en}}</ref> Commentators also remark that these benefits (''InDepthNews'' dubs the relationship "software-based guarantee") come with strategic dependency for the Ukraine side.<ref name="IDN"/><ref name="carnegie"/><ref>{{cite news |last1=Mysyshyn |first1=Anna |title=Advanced Technologies in the War in Ukraine: Risks for Democracy and Human Rights {{!}} German Marshall Fund of the United States |url=https://www.gmfus.org/sites/default/files/2024-10/Mysyshyn%20-%20Ukraine%20war%20tech%20-%20paper.pdf |work=www.gmfus.org |date=30 September 2024 |language=en}}</ref> In June 2026, analyst [[Markus Reisner]] said that Ukraine had gained the upper hand in its war with Russia through the use of Palantir. Reisner said Ukraine had become a laboratory for Palantir to test and develop AI capabilities, with implications for the US's effort to prevent a war in Taiwan.<ref>{{cite news |last1= Niemeyer |first1= Frauke |title= "Mit KI erleben wir einen Evolutionsschritt im Krieg" |url= https://www.n-tv.de/politik/Mit-KI-erleben-wir-einen-Evolutionsschritt-im-Krieg-id30943035.html |work= n-tv.de |date= 8 June 2026 |language= de}}</ref>

===Netherlands===
In 2025, Dutch Justice Minister [[David van Weel]] publicly confirmed that Dutch police departments had used Palantir software since 2011, a use that had remained largely undisclosed. Over the subsequent decade, researchers seeking government transparency were repeatedly denied, with documents finally released only after litigation but with 99% of content redacted. They show that many contracts with Palantir were signed.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hugo |first1=Hayte |title=Minister bevestigt dat Nederland al jarenlang software van Palantir gebruikt |url=https://tweakers.net/nieuws/238366/minister-bevestigt-dat-nederland-al-jarenlang-software-van-palantir-gebruikt.html |work=Tweakers |date=August 22, 2025 |language=NL}}</ref>

===Israel===
{{See also|List of companies involved in the Gaza war}}
Palantir's London office was the target of demonstrations by [[Gaza war protests in the United Kingdom|pro-Palestine protesters]] in December 2023 after it was awarded a large contract to manage NHS data. The protesters accused Palantir of being complicit in [[Israeli war crimes in the Gaza war]] because it provides the [[Israel Defence Force]] (IDF) with intelligence and surveillance services, including a form of [[predictive policing]].<ref name="ind1223" /> In January 2024, Palantir agreed to a strategic partnership with the IDF under which it would provide the IDF with services to assist its "war-related missions".<ref>{{cite web |last=Newman |first=Marissa |title=Thiel's Palantir, Israel Agree Strategic Partnership for Battle Tech |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-01-12/palantir-israel-agree-to-strategic-partnership-for-battle-tech?leadSource=uverify%20wall |website=Bloomberg |access-date=January 16, 2024 |date=January 12, 2024}}</ref> Karp has been emphatic in his public support for Israel. He has frequently criticized what he calls the inaction of other tech leaders. His position has prompted several employees to leave Palantir.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Koller |first=Alex |date=March 13, 2024 |title=Palantir CEO says his outspoken pro-Israel views have caused employees to leave company |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2024/03/13/palantir-ceo-says-outspoken-pro-israel-views-led-employees-to-leave-.html |access-date=October 7, 2024 |publisher=CNBC}}</ref>

In 2024, Irish politician and former Palantir employee [[Eoin Hayes]] was suspended by his party, the [[Social Democrats (Ireland)|Social Democrats]], for saying at a press conference that he had sold shares in Palantir before he entered politics, when he had sold the shares a month after being elected as a city councillor. Hayes corrected the date in a statement issued that day and provided additional details later.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Officer |first=Press |date=December 12, 2024 |title=Statement from Eoin Hayes TD on his previous employment with Palantir - Social Democrats |url=https://www.socialdemocrats.ie/statement-from-eoin-hayes-td-on-his-previous-employment-with-palantir/,%20https://www.socialdemocrats.ie/statement-from-eoin-hayes-td-on-his-previous-employment-with-palantir/ |access-date=July 8, 2025 |website=SocialDemocrats.ie}}{{Dead link|date=March 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=December 10, 2024 |title='I let myself and the party down': Suspended Soc Dems TD Eoin Hayes says he will take his seat in Dáil as Independent |url=https://www.independent.ie/irish-news/i-let-myself-and-the-party-down-suspended-soc-dems-td-eoin-hayes-says-he-will-take-his-seat-in-dail-as-independent/a1520818353.html |access-date=June 15, 2025 |website=Irish Independent |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=December 11, 2024 |title=Eoin Hayes to sit as independent after suspension by Social Democrats |url=https://www.rte.ie/news/politics/2024/1210/1485698-eoin-hayes-soc-dems/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250116182644/https://www.rte.ie/news/politics/2024/1210/1485698-eoin-hayes-soc-dems/ |archive-date=January 16, 2025 |access-date=February 6, 2025 |publisher=RTE}}</ref> The Social Democrats criticized the [[Israeli invasion of the Gaza Strip]] and accused Hayes of "profiting from genocide".<ref>{{Cite web |date=December 10, 2024 |title='I let myself and the party down': Suspended Soc Dems TD Eoin Hayes says he will take his seat in Dáil as Independent |url=https://www.independent.ie/irish-news/i-let-myself-and-the-party-down-suspended-soc-dems-td-eoin-hayes-says-he-will-take-his-seat-in-dail-as-independent/a1520818353.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241213152647/https://www.independent.ie/irish-news/i-let-myself-and-the-party-down-suspended-soc-dems-td-eoin-hayes-says-he-will-take-his-seat-in-dail-as-independent/a1520818353.html |archive-date=December 13, 2024 |access-date=February 6, 2025 |website=Irish Independent}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Marsi |first=Federica |title=UN report lists companies complicit in Israel's "genocide": Who are they? |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/7/1/un-report-lists-companies-complicit-in-israels-genocide-who-are-they |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250730193011/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/7/1/un-report-lists-companies-complicit-in-israels-genocide-who-are-they |archive-date=2025-07-30 |access-date=2026-04-22 |work=Al Jazeera |language=en}}</ref> In May 2025, a pro-Palestinian protest was held in Denver against Congressman [[Jason Crow]] for repeatedly accepting campaign donations from Palantir executives.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Metzger |first1=Hannah |title=Protesters Call Out Jason Crow Over Campaign Donors With Ties to ICE, Israel |url=https://www.westword.com/news/protest-disrupts-jason-crow-town-hall-donor-ties-ice-israel-24659385/ |work=[[Westword]] |date=May 29, 2025}}</ref>

===Switzerland===
In late 2025, the left-leaning Swiss publication ''Republik'' revealed internal reports by the Swiss federal government that Switzerland had been courted as a client of Palantir since 2018. Palantir has offices in [[Zurich]] and [[Bern]], and the Swiss Defence Department through [[Armasuisse]] was initially interested in buying Palantir's information systems, but larger deals with the Swiss government were rejected out of concern that data could be released to U.S. intelligence agencies. The publication also mentioned a possible reputational risk if Switzerland had adopted such surveillance software. Later it was made public that Palantir attempted to sue ''Republik'' for not having the right to reply to the allegations.<ref>Fichter A (December 2025)[https://www.republik.ch/2026/02/18/how-tenaciously-palantir-courted-switzerland "How tenaciously Palantir courted Switzerland] ''Republik''. Retrieved 23 February 2026.</ref><ref> Ruehl M (February 2026)[https://www.ft.com/content/434b6d98-83d1-4ba1-a929-150341bcaea4 "Palantir sues magazine that revealed Switzerland rejected its approaches"] ''Financial Times'' Retrieved 23 February 2026.</ref>  

===Spain===
In July 2026, it was reported that the Spanish government has been directing government agencies and companies with stakes controlled by [[Sociedad Estatal de Participaciones Industriales|the sovereign wealth fund SEPI]] to avoid Palantir. A prospective contract with the company [[Navantia]] was reportedly blocked after the government interfered. Despite this, Palantir was awarded a contract with the Armed Forces Intelligence Center in 2023, and military commanders have reportedly defended the relationship with the company.<ref>{{cite news |title= Spain also stops Peter Thiel: Moncloa asks public companies not to contract with the giant Palantir |url= https://www.democrata.es/en/digital-ai/spain-also-stops-peter-thiel-moncloa-asks-public-companies-not-to-contract-with-the-giant-palantir/ |work= Demócrata |date= 1 July 2026 |language= en}}</ref>

===Italy===
According to a July 2026 report by Turkey's ''Clash report'', the government of Italy has been increasingly skeptical of a relationship with Palantir, and Palantir's only confirmed government contract in Italy is a minor, open-source data integration contract with the ministry of defense.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Koçak |first1= Ahmet |title= Italy Quietly Distances Itself From Palantir as European Skepticism Grows |url=https://clashreport.com/world/articles/italy-quietly-distances-itself-from-palantir-as-european-skepticism-grows-tjl7vc9vkwo |work= Clash Report |date= 2 July 2026 |language= en}}</ref> A 2026 report by ''[[Alberto Alemanno#List of initiatives|The good lobby]]'' notes that there is little transparency on the matter, but government documents show the existence of a series of contracts since 2015. The ministry of defense refused to answer questions about a 2024 initiative on "supply of the Palantir Gotham license", saying the information was classified.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dominante |first1=Bianca |title=Peter Thiel porta Palantir al ministero della Difesa |url=https://www.thegoodlobby.it/peter-thiel-palantir-difesa-crosetto/?__cf_chl_f_tk=21UTTWuRzyY_vxhoxwiICep7SZiF0R79nzWb9c0dmWg-1783006683-1.0.1.1-Io4Ly3OJysHSZ9wQ3MUPJoqTELyFPg1uLazc9OuioCQ |work= The Good Lobby Italia |date= 27 March 2026 |language=it}}</ref>

===Canada===
The military forces started a contract with Palantir in 2020. 2025 documents showed that the contract's value has increased over the years.<ref>{{cite news |title=Documents reveal that Palantir contract was worth $30M more than government disclosed |url=https://www.thestar.com/politics/federal/documents-reveal-that-palantir-contract-was-worth-30m-more-than-government-disclosed/article_360cfa78-7ada-4b82-a06d-3e0287eb4c8d.html |work=The Toronto Star |date=4 June 2026 |language=en-CA}}</ref> According to [[Public Services and Procurement Canada]], 18 Palantir employees have received security clearance and access to sensitive data under Contract Security Program since March 28, 2019.<ref>{{cite news |title=Ottawa a autorisé 18 employés de Palantir à accéder à des documents de défense classifiés |url=https://ici.radio-canada.ca/nouvelle/2260320/palantir-autorisation-federale-documents-defense-classifies |work=Radio-Canada |date=10 June 2026 |language=fr-ca}}</ref> Cyber analysts at [[Information Warfare Monitor]], a Canadian public-private venture that operated from 2003 to 2012, used Palantir Gotham.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bennett |first=Nicole M. |date=August 27, 2025 |title=When the government can see everything: How one company – Palantir – is mapping the nation's data |url=http://theconversation.com/when-the-government-can-see-everything-how-one-company-palantir-is-mapping-the-nations-data-263178 |access-date=September 16, 2025 |website=The Conversation |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=August 24, 2025 |title=Palantir's tools pose an invisible danger we are just beginning to comprehend |url=https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2025/aug/24/palantir-artificial-intelligence-civil-rights |access-date=September 16, 2025 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=May 12, 2009 |title=Tracking Cyberspies Through the Web Wilderness (Published 2009) |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2009/05/12/science/12cyber.html |access-date=September 16, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> 

===Ecuador===
Ecuador's {{ill|National Customs Service (SENAE)|es|Servicio Nacional de Aduanas (Ecuador)}} signed a contract with Palantir in May 2025, targeting drug trafficking and smuggling.<ref>{{cite news |last1= Reporters |first1=RT Staff |title=Ecuador Deploys Palantir’s A.I. in High-Stakes War Against Drug Smugglers |url=https://www.riotimesonline.com/ecuador-deploys-palantirs-a-i-in-high-stakes-war-against-drug-smugglers/?__cf_chl_f_tk=FF4TUXHMzaQ8_6RQsqXysmERhtcRcE.JPD6PEBRrj7c-1783010709-1.0.1.1-4bKU50mBTorX5qt7I2_VeD4zleikYNR1vH2j3nPXX.Q |work=The Rio Times |date=30 May 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

===Brazil===
While the federal government's Transparency Portal does not show any contract with Palantir, in 2024, the {{ill|National Fund for the Development of Education|pt|Fundo Nacional de Desenvolvimento da Educação}} mentioned a "success story that modernized the transfer of public funds from the agency to the federated entities", which an executive of Palantir Brazil confirmed.<ref name="dwpt">{{cite news |last1=Ehl |first1= David |title=O que diz o manifesto da Palantir, que promove "armas de IA" |url=https://www.dw.com/pt-br/o-que-diz-o-manifesto-da-palantir-que-promove-armas-de-ia/a-76897062 |work=dw.com |date=22 April 2026 |language=pt}}</ref> After a September 2025 meeting of members of the Special Committee on Artificial Intelligence of the Chamber of Deputies and Palantir in the US, federal deputy [[Rui Falcão]] asked the ministries of [[Ministry of Management and Innovation in Public Services|management]] and [[Ministry of Education (Brazil)|education]] to clarify which public bodies use Palantir's software, but got no answer.<ref name= "dwpt"/>

=== Australia ===
The [[Australian Defence Force]] has been using Palantir since 2011. In February 2026, the Cyber and Electronic Warfare Division signed a contract that secured local military use of Palantir's ICT System Platform.<ref>{{cite news |last1= Gregoire |first1= Paul |title= Australian Integration into Palantir's Global Surveillance Panopticon Is Not Inevitable |url= https://www.sydneycriminallawyers.com.au/blog/australian-integration-into-palantirs-global-surveillance-panopticon-is-not-inevitable/ |work= Sydney Criminal Lawyers |date= 12 July 2026 |language= en-AU}}</ref>

=== Other ===
The [[International Atomic Energy Agency]] (IAEA) used Palantir to verify whether Iran was in compliance with the [[Iran nuclear deal framework|2015 agreement]].<ref name="IranNuclearDeal" />

==Partnerships with corporations==
===United States===
Palantir's partnership with [[Anduril Industries]] extends to many areas. In later 2024, there were reportedly building and leading a consortium with another dozen contractors, including [[SpaceX]], [[OpenAI]], and Saronic, to challenge the domination of the traditional "primes".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kinder |first1=Tabby |last2=Hammond |first2=George |title=Palantir and Anduril join forces with tech groups to bid for Pentagon contracts |url=https://www.ft.com/content/6cfdfe2b-6872-4963-bde8-dc6c43be5093 |work=Financial Times |date=December 22, 2024 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250216003330/https://www.ft.com/content/6cfdfe2b-6872-4963-bde8-dc6c43be5093 |archive-date=February 16, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Sharwood |first1=Simon |title=Palantir and Anduril form partnership, as Sauron funded |url=https://www.theregister.com/2024/12/09/palantir_anduril_alliance/ |date=December 9, 2024 |language=en}}</ref> They also partner with EVTOL company [[Archer Aviation]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Root |first1=Al |title=Archer Aviation Stock Soars. It's Working With Palantir. |url=https://www.barrons.com/articles/archer-aviation-stock-palantir-6b1619dc |work=Barron's |date=May 13, 2025 |language=en-us}}</ref> Alongside [[8VC]] and [[General Catalyst]], Palantir also helped create and is a member of the New American Industrial Alliance.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Jeans |first1=David |title=Trump Wants Manufacturing Jobs Back. Silicon Valley Is Lining Up To Help |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/davidjeans/2024/12/11/new-american-industrial-alliance-trump-silicon-valley/ |work=Forbes |date=December 11, 2024 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20241211140242/https://www.forbes.com/sites/davidjeans/2024/12/11/new-american-industrial-alliance-trump-silicon-valley/ |archive-date=December 11, 2024 |language=en}}</ref><ref name="ft1">{{cite news |last1=Tabby |first1=Kinder |last2=Murchu |first2=Cynthia |title=Palantir's 'revolving door' with government spurs huge growth |url=https://www.ft.com/content/a65c93af-abf9-427c-a2ee-a9f5b3d518b9 |work=Financial Times |date=February 7, 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250208133234/https://www.ft.com/content/a65c93af-abf9-427c-a2ee-a9f5b3d518b9 |archive-date=February 8, 2025}}</ref> The other members are other defense contractors and hardtech companies, such as Anduril, [[Andreessen Horowitz]], Hadrian, [[Oklo Inc.]], [[Narya Capital]], [[Booz Allen]], [[Joby Aviation]], Regent Craft, Atomic Industries, Impulse Space, and Dirac, which hope to reduce government regulations.<ref>{{cite web |title= Inside Palantir's Expanding Influence Operation |url=https://www.techtransparencyproject.org/articles/inside-palantirs-expanding-influence-operation |publisher=Tech Transparency Project |access-date=October 18, 2025 |language=en-us}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Our Mission |publisher=NAIA |url=https://newindustrials.org/our-mission/ |access-date=October 18, 2025}}</ref>

Palantir, [[TWG Global]], and [[xAI (company)|xAI]] have a joint venture.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Silverman |first1=Jacob |title=Elon Musk's AI Grift |url=https://www.thenation.com/article/world/elon-musk-ai-saudi-funding/ |date=September 12, 2025}}</ref> Palantir collaborates with [[Anthropic]] and [[Amazon Web Services]] to provide Anthropic's [[Claude (language model)|Claude]] AI models to U.S. intelligence and defense agencies.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Rosenberg |first1=Scott |title=Anthropic, Palantir, Amazon team up on defense AI |url=https://www.axios.com/2024/11/08/anthropic-palantir-amazon-claude-defense-ai |work=Axios |date=November 8, 2024 |language=en}}</ref> It also partners with the traditional defense "primes": [[Boeing]] (specifically its defense and space unit),<ref>{{cite news |last1=Jain |first1=Aishwarya |title=Boeing's defense and space unit partners with Palantir for AI adoption |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/aerospace-defense/boeings-defense-space-unit-partners-with-palantir-ai-adoption-2025-09-23/ |date=September 24, 2025}}</ref> [[Lockheed Martin]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=Eckstein |first1=Megan |title=Palantir, Lockheed Martin team up to modernize naval combat systems |url=https://www.defensenews.com/industry/techwatch/2022/11/30/palantir-lockheed-martin-team-up-to-modernize-naval-combat-systems/ |work=Defense News |date=November 30, 2022 |language=en}}</ref> [[Northrop Grumman]] (TITAN system),<ref>{{cite news |last1=Subin |first1=Samantha |title=Palantir delivers first two AI-enabled systems to U.S. Army |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2025/03/07/palantir-delivers-first-two-ai-enabled-systems-to-us-army.html |publisher=CNBC |date=March 7, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> and [[General Dynamics]] (through General Dynamics Land System).<ref>{{cite news |last1=McMiler |first1=Arthur |title=General Dynamics Units Form Partnership on Combat Software |url=https://www.executivebiz.com/articles/general-dynamics-software-development-partnerships |work=executivebiz.com |date=March 27, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250719082921/https://www.executivebiz.com/articles/general-dynamics-software-development-partnerships |archive-date=July 19, 2025}}</ref>

Palantir is known to disrupt the traditional consulting business model.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Schumpeter |title=Who needs Accenture in the age of AI? |url=https://www.economist.com/business/2025/06/26/who-needs-accenture-in-the-age-of-ai |newspaper=The Economist |date=June 26, 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250713235411/https://www.economist.com/business/2025/06/26/who-needs-accenture-in-the-age-of-ai |archive-date=July 13, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |last1=Kelly |first1=Makena |title=Palantir Is Extending Its Reach Even Further Into Government |url=https://www.wired.com/story/palantir-government-contracting-push/ |magazine=Wired |date=August 1, 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250804124337/https://www.wired.com/story/palantir-government-contracting-push/ |archive-date=August 4, 2025}}</ref> It also partners with traditional companies (and rivals in government contract) such as [[Deloitte]], Booz Allen, and [[Accenture]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kline |first1=Alan |title=Booz Allen teams with tech giant Palantir to drive innovation for U.S. military |url=https://www.bizjournals.com/washington/news/2024/12/06/booz-allen-hamilton-palantir-collaboration.html |work=bizjournals |date=December 6, 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Collins |first1=Elodie |title=Deloitte, Palantir Jointly Develop EOS |url=https://www.executivebiz.com/articles/deloitte-palantir-strategic-alliance-enterprise-operating-system-ai |work=ExecutiveBiz |date=July 22, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250727053613/https://www.executivebiz.com/articles/deloitte-palantir-strategic-alliance-enterprise-operating-system-ai |archive-date=July 27, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Jamison |first1=Miles |title=Palantir, Accenture Seek to Boost US Gov't Ops With AI |url=https://www.executivebiz.com/articles/palantir-accenture-federal-services-partnership-federal-operations-ai |work=executivebiz.com |date=July 1, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250704030808/https://www.executivebiz.com/articles/palantir-accenture-federal-services-partnership-federal-operations-ai |archive-date=July 4, 2025 |language=en-us}}</ref> On February 8, 2021, Palantir and [[IBM]] announced a partnership that would use [[IBM]]'s hybrid cloud data platform alongside Palantir's operations platform for building applications. The product, Palantir for IBM Cloud Pak for Data, was expected to simplify the process of building and deploying AI-integrated applications with [[IBM Watson]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://newsroom.ibm.com/2021-02-08-IBM-and-Palantir-Partner-to-Help-Businesses-Easily-Deploy-Powerful-and-Open-AI-Applications-Unlock-Valuable-Data-Across-Hybrid-Cloud-Environments|title=IBM and Palantir Partner to Help Businesses Easily Deploy Powerful and Open AI Applications; Unlock Valuable Data Across Hybrid Cloud Environments|website=IBM Newsroom|access-date=April 5, 2021|archive-date=March 24, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210324155337/https://newsroom.ibm.com/2021-02-08-IBM-and-Palantir-Partner-to-Help-Businesses-Easily-Deploy-Powerful-and-Open-AI-Applications-Unlock-Valuable-Data-Across-Hybrid-Cloud-Environments|url-status=live}}</ref> On March 5, 2021, Palantir announced its partnership with [[Amazon AWS]]. Palantir's [[Enterprise resource planning|ERP Suite]] was optimized to run on [[Amazon Web Services]]. [[BP]] used the ERP suite.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://blog.palantir.com/launching-erp-suite-to-deliver-cost-savings-for-aws-customers-ceed6e6dd927|title=Launching ERP Suite to deliver cost savings for AWS customers|date=March 5, 2021|access-date=April 5, 2021|archive-date=April 19, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210419142041/https://blog.palantir.com/launching-erp-suite-to-deliver-cost-savings-for-aws-customers-ceed6e6dd927|url-status=live}}</ref> On August 8, 2024, Palantir and Microsoft announced a partnership whereby Palantir will deploy its suite of products on [[Microsoft Azure]] Government clouds.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Haselton |first=Todd |date=August 8, 2024 |title=Palantir jumps 11% on Microsoft partnership to sell AI to U.S. defense, intel agencies |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2024/08/08/palantir-jumps-on-microsoft-partnership-to-sell-ai-to-us-defense.html |access-date=August 10, 2024 |publisher=CNBC |language=en |archive-date=August 9, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240809232347/https://www.cnbc.com/2024/08/08/palantir-jumps-on-microsoft-partnership-to-sell-ai-to-us-defense.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Microsoft, Palantir Partner on AI for U.S. Gov't Agencies |url=https://rcpmag.com/Articles/2024/08/12/Microsoft-Palantir-AI-Agencies.aspx |access-date=March 17, 2025 |website=Redmond Channel Partner |language=en-US}}</ref>

===Korea===
In Korea, Palantir has strategic relationships with [[HD Hyundai Heavy Industries|HD Hyundai]] and [[KT Corporation|KT]]. It has a data alliance with HD Hyundai and partners with HD Hyundai and Siemens to automate shipbuilding for both Korea and the U.S.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ki |first1=Hyun |title=HD Hyundai Partners with Palantir, Siemens for Shipyard Automation |url=https://koreatechtoday.com/hd-hyundai-partners-with-palantir-siemens-for-shipyard-automation/ |work=KoreaTechToday - Korea's Leading Tech and Startup Media Platform |date=February 28, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Park |first1=Soo-nam |title=[Park Soo-nam's CEO Analysis 8] Behind the HD Hyundai-Palantir 'Data Alliance': Chung Kisun Bets the Future of Korea's Defense Industry on the U.S. Security System |url=https://www.ceomagazine.co.kr/news/articleView.html?idxno=34136 |date=September 23, 2025}}</ref> KT cooperates with Palantir to scale Foundry and AIP across Korean industries.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ga-eul |title=Inside KT and Palantir's Next Move: Building the Future of AI-Powered Enterprises in Korea |url=https://koreatechtoday.com/inside-kt-and-palantirs-next-move-building-the-future-of-ai-powered-enterprises-in-korea/ |work=KoreaTechToday - Korea's Leading Tech and Startup Media Platform |date=October 14, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Ye-eun |first1=Jie |title=KT, Palantir chiefs discuss AI expansion in Seoul |url=https://www.koreaherald.com/article/10593096 |work=The Korea Herald |date=October 14, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> In 2025, Samsung partnered with Palantir to increase the yield and quality of its chips.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Chang |first1=Ollie |title=Samsung taps CIA-born Palantir AI to rescue faltering chip yields |url=https://www.digitimes.com/news/a20250319PD241/samsung-production-2024-saas-data.html |work=DigiTimes |date=March 20, 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

===Germany===
[[SAP]] has strategic partnerships with Palantir and [[Perplexity AI|Perplexity]], aiming to enhance SAP's business AI offerings.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lin |first1=Ines |last2=Hsiao |first2=Jingyue |title=SAP partners with Perplexity and Palantir to accelerate business AI ecosystem |url=https://www.digitimes.com/news/a20250522PD231/germany-software-ai-agent-cloud-business.html |work=DigiTimes |date=May 23, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> Palantir also partners with SAP's U.S.-based branch, SAP National Security Services, to offer AI systems to the U.S. public sector.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Jamison |first1=Miles |title=SAP NS2, Palantir Bring AI-Driven Systems to Public Sector |url=https://www.executivebiz.com/articles/sap-ns2-palantir-partner-ai-driven-systems-public-sector |work=executivebiz.com |date=May 21, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250926100941/https://www.executivebiz.com/articles/sap-ns2-palantir-partner-ai-driven-systems-public-sector |archive-date=September 26, 2025}}</ref> Palantir has a partnership with [[Axel Springer SE]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=Jirát |first1=Von Jan |title=Palantir Technologies: Fauler Datenzauber am Zürichsee |url=https://www.woz.ch/2116/palantir-technologies/fauler-datenzauber-am-zuerichsee |work=woz.ch |date=April 21, 2021 |language=de}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Big data platform supports publishing house with data processing |url=https://pcg.io/insights/big-data-platform-supports-publishing-house/#:~:text=Axel%20Springer%20has%20been%20migrating,platform%20and%20describes%20itself%20as |website=pcg.io |access-date=October 18, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> a publisher known to have a close relationship with Karp, who sat on its board,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Glover |first1=George |title=Palantir CEO Alex Karp says he loves 'burning the short-sellers' who bet against 'great companies' to fund their cocaine habits |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/palantir-ceo-alex-karp-short-sellers-buy-cocaine-stock-market-2024-3 |work=Business Insider |date=March 14, 2024}}</ref> and Thiel, who employed Springer's [[Mathias Döpfner]]'s son Moritz as his chief of staff and business associate. Some German media outlets have speculated about Thiel's political influence in Germany through his relationships with media executives and his investment in emerging political figures.<ref>{{cite news |last1=magazin |first1=Jonas Rest, manager |title=(m+) Peter Thiel: 50 Millionen Dollar für den Risikokapitalfonds von Mathias Döpfners Sohn |url=https://www.manager-magazin.de/unternehmen/tech/peter-thiel-50-millionen-dollar-fuer-den-risikokapitalfonds-von-mathias-doepfners-sohn-a-238af84f-d3bf-49e6-b99f-95568a215b95 |work=manager-magazin.de |date=February 22, 2025 |language=de}}</ref> Palantir partners with [[Merck KGaA]] through joint ventures Syntrophy, which focuses on cancer data analytics, and Athinia, which focuses on the semiconductor sector.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Burger |first1=Ludwig |last2=Weiss |first2=Patricia |title=Palantir to offer cancer analytics under JV deal with Germany's Merck |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-palantir-merck-kgaa-jv/palantir-to-offer-cancer-analytics-under-jv-deal-with-germanys-merck-idUSKCN1NO1KH/ |date=November 20, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Nellis |first1=Stephen |title=German chip chemical supplier to spend $1 bln in U.S., pairs with Palantir on supply chain data |url=https://www.reuters.com/markets/deals/german-chip-chemical-supplier-spend-1-bln-us-pairs-with-palantir-supply-chain-2021-12-07/ |date=December 7, 2021 |language=en}}</ref> Palantir is strategic partners with [[Helsing (company)|Helsing]].<ref name="arctic" /> [[Espreso TV]] has linked the unusual number of defense CEOs (Brian Schimpf of Anduril Industries, Gundbert Scherf of Helsing, and Karp) at the [[2025 Bilderberg Conference]] to Thiel's influence.<ref name="espreso" />

===United Arab Emirates===
On November 4, 2025, a joint venture between Palantir and [[Dubai Holding]], the Dubai government's investment arm, was announced. ''[[The New Arab]]'' noted that the partnership came just a day after Microsoft revealed plans to invest $15.2 billion in AI and cloud computing projects across the UAE.<ref>{{cite news |last1= The New Arab Staff & Agencies |title= US AI firm Palantir announces joint venture in Dubai |url= https://www.newarab.com/news/us-ai-firm-palantir-announces-joint-venture-dubai |date= November 4, 2025}}</ref>

==Controversies and conspiracy theories==
Palantir has been criticized for its role in expanding [[Surveillance|government surveillance]] using [[artificial intelligence]] and [[Facial recognition system|facial recognition software]].<ref name=":5">{{Cite web |last=Hvistendahl |first=Mara |date=January 30, 2021 |title=How the LAPD and Palantir Use Data to Justify Racist Policing |url=https://theintercept.com/2021/01/30/lapd-palantir-data-driven-policing/ |access-date=June 24, 2025 |website=The Intercept |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name=":6">{{Cite news |last=Allyn |first=Bobby |date=May 3, 2025 |title=How Palantir, the secretive tech company, is rising in the Trump era |url=https://www.npr.org/2025/05/01/nx-s1-5372776/palantir-tech-contracts-trump |access-date=June 24, 2025 |publisher=NPR |language=en}}</ref> Some former employees and outside observers have also criticized the company's contracts under the [[Second presidency of Donald Trump|second Trump Administration]], which enable [[Deportation in the second presidency of Donald Trump|deportations]] and the aggregation of [[Information sensitivity|sensitive data]] on Americans across [[Government agency|administrative agencies]].<ref name=":7">{{Cite web |date=May 30, 2025 |title=Trump appears to be building an unprecedented spy machine that could track Americans |url=https://www.msnbc.com/top-stories/latest/trump-palantir-surveillance-americans-rcna210017 |access-date=June 24, 2025 |website=MSNBC |language=en}}</ref><ref name="nyt"/> 

''Wired'' wrote that some people think Palantir "maintains a giant, centralized database of information collected from all of its clients", which is untrue. The company's work does not affect how each organization collects its data but helps organizations "integrate and analyze data without needing to fix the underlying architecture", effectively functioning like "a technical band-aid". Palantir's customers "need to already have the data they want to work with—Palantir itself does not provide any".<ref name="HaskinsWired" /> Citing a former Palantir employee, ''Wired'' reported that the government can rely on Gotham to "centralize everything an agency knows about a person in one place".<ref name="HaskinsWired">{{cite news |last1=Haskins |first1=Caroline |title=What Does Palantir Actually Do? |url=https://www.wired.com/story/palantir-what-the-company-does/ |access-date=October 13, 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250907214447/https://www.wired.com/story/palantir-what-the-company-does/|archive-date=September 7, 2025}}</ref> 

Others observe that Palantir does not collect or store data, but only helps analyze data that customers have already collected and provides services and platforms where customers retain the rights; still, it is generally accepted that abuses by governments and data management failures can happen.<ref name="HaskinsWired">{{cite news |last1=Haskins |first1=Caroline |title=What Does Palantir Actually Do? |url=https://www.wired.com/story/palantir-what-the-company-does/ |access-date=October 13, 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250907214447/https://www.wired.com/story/palantir-what-the-company-does/|archive-date=September 7, 2025}}</ref><ref name="logic">{{cite news |last1=Hemmadi |first1=Murad |title=The Defence Department had a $14M contract with Palantir for data management software |url=https://thelogic.co/news/palantir-department-of-national-defence-contract-canada/ |quote=Palantir had no access to the information processed by its software, the department claimed in response to questions from The Logic. Gotham was only used on data protected on 'closed, classified' National Defence networks 'within secure facilities', department spokesperson Andrée-Anne Poulin said|date=September 18, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Frequently asked questions (FAQs) |url=https://www.england.nhs.uk/digitaltechnology/nhs-federated-data-platform/fdp-faqs/#can-palantir-profit-from-nhs-data |publisher=NHS Federated Data Platform Frequently asked questions (FAQs) |access-date=October 13, 2025 |quote=Palantir has no intellectual property rights (IPRs) in NHS data as part of this contract. Where NHS England commissions material containing IPRs, it owns those IPRs – this includes the data ontology commissioned by NHS England to support NHS FDP use known as the Canonical Data Model}}</ref><ref name="df">{{cite news |last1=Loll |first1=Anna |title=Palantir in Deutschland - Datenschützer warnen vor Polizei-Software aus den USA |url=https://www.deutschlandfunk.de/palantir-deutschland-polizei-software-datenschutz-100.html |work=Deutschlandfunk |date=June 5, 2025 |language=de}}</ref>

The researchers Andrew Iliadis and Amelia Acker wrote, "lines of critique concerning hypothetical data sourcing can be defended", but Palantir "remains open to the critique of potentially being an accessory to acts of deportation, imprisonment, and racism through its contracts". They also wrote that Palantir is in contact with sensitive data through its forward-deployed software engineers, and that the clients use Palantir's platform to track and collect data, as shown in Palantir's patents.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Iliadis |first1=Andrew |last2=Acker |first2=Amelia |title=The seer and the seen: Surveying Palantir's surveillance platform |journal=www.tandfonline.com |date=19 August 2021 |doi=10.1080/01972243.2022.2100851 |url=https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/01972243.2022.2100851#d1e286|hdl=20.500.12613/8070 |hdl-access=free }}</ref> 

Incidents of data leaks or mismanagement have been noted, including the 2015 data leak in which ''Techcrunch'' received documents that revealed the tools Palantir used and a list of key customers;<ref>{{cite news |last1=Burns |first1=Matt |title=Leaked Palantir Doc Reveals Uses, Specific Functions And Key Clients |url=https://techcrunch.com/2015/01/11/leaked-palantir-doc-reveals-uses-specific-functions-and-key-clients/ |work=TechCrunch |date=January 12, 2015}}</ref> the 2021 software misconfiguration that allowed some FBI employees unwarranted access;<ref>{{cite news |last1=Burgess |first1=Christopher |title=Palantir Misconfiguration Allowed Unauthorized FBI Access |url=https://securityboulevard.com/2021/09/palantir-misconfiguration-allowed-unauthorized-fbi-access/ |work=Security Boulevard |date=September 7, 2021}}</ref> and the 2025 lawsuit Palantir filed against former employees Radha Jain and Joanna Cohen for stealing data to launch a "copycat" company with the help of [[General Catalyst]] (the two had been entrusted with the source code and Cohen was noted to have interacted with Palantir's important customers).<ref>{{cite news |last1=Van Boris |first1=Bob |title=Palantir Says Top AI Engineers Stole Secrets for 'Copycat' Firm |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-10-30/palantir-says-top-ai-engineers-stole-secrets-for-copycat-firm |archive-url=https://archive.today/20251030154035/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-10-30/palantir-says-top-ai-engineers-stole-secrets-for-copycat-firm |archive-date=October 30, 2025}}</ref> In December 2025, Palantir expanded its lawsuit to include the CEO of Percepta AI.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Albert |first=Victoria |date=December 11, 2025 |title=Palantir Sues CEO of Rival AI Firm, Alleges Widespread Effort to Poach Employees |url=https://www.wsj.com/business/palantir-sues-ceo-of-rival-ai-firm-alleges-widespread-effort-to-poach-employees-9c297986 |access-date=December 17, 2025 |website=The Wall Street Journal |language=en-US}}</ref>

Some commentators have said that many conspiracy theories about Palantir have arisen, partly due to the CIA's early involvement and Palantir's initial focus on intelligence contracts, but also partly because the founders, including Thiel and Karp, deliberately crafted an image that exaggerated Palantir's relationship with the CIA or its capacity during its early phase.<ref name= "BrillFortune">{{cite news |last1= Brill |first1= Steven |title= Trump, Palantir, and the Battle to Clean Up a Huge Army Procurement Swamp |url= https://fortune.com/longform/palantir-pentagon-trump/ |work= Fortune |date= 27 March 2017 |language= en}}</ref><ref name= "King">{{cite book |last1= King |first1= Anthony |title= AI, Automation, and War: The Rise of a Military-Tech Complex |date= 19 August 2025 |publisher= Princeton University Press |isbn= 978-0-691-26515-5 |page=87 |url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/AI_Automation_and_War/tpJIEQAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22palantir%22+%22total+information+awareness%22&pg=PA87 |language= en}}</ref><ref name= "AldenBuzzfeed"/> It was reported that Palantir's technological phalanx was crucial in finding [[Osama bin Laden]] in 2011.<ref>{{Cite news |last= Finn |first= Holly |date= February 23, 2013 |title= The Humane Way to Crack Terrorists |url= https://www.palantir.com/assets/xrfr7uokpv1b/78jryCcZDq1foX4FiNBxkg/d49446fb1dc24b75b0a92013928da059/WSJ_HumaneWayToCrackTerrorists.pdf |access-date= 3 June 2026 |work= The Wall Street Journal}}</ref> But according to Anthony King, an expert on war studies who has worked with Royal Marine and NATO,<ref>{{cite web |title= Discovery |url= https://experts.exeter.ac.uk/109-anthony-king |website= experts.exeter.ac.uk |access-date= 18 July 2026}}</ref> the story was at best an exaggeration. King notes that this story about Bin Laden as well as the connection with the [[Total Information Awareness]] program were propaganda Thiel used to consolidate the small foothold Palantir had in the defense forces by 2011.<ref name= "King"/> In 2021, ''[[The New Republic]]'' wrote that even when Bloomberg ran an article (with the headline "PALANTIR KNOWS EVERYTHING ABOUT YOU" (2018)) that exaggerated the privacy concerns, Palantir's leaders did nothing, because the story helped raise Palantir's valuation, which reached $40 billion by January 2021.<ref name= "WeigelNR">{{cite news |last1= Weigel |first1= Moira |title= The Making of Peter Thiel's Networks |url= https://newrepublic.com/article/164768/peter-thiel-networks-contrarian-book-review |work= The New Republic |date= 20 December 2021}}</ref>

On the other hand, there were occasions when Palantir's capabilities were underestimated due to non-technical factors like culture clash. In 2012, the [[United States Army Test and Evaluation Command|Army Test and Evaluation Command]] (ATEC) produced a positive report about Palantir and recommended the Army install its system but was ordered to modify this report due to sensitivities "about purchased systems in use that came outside the normal acquisitions process".<ref name= "BrillFortune"/> In 2013, the U.S. Army canceled an independent [[Mitre Corporation|MITRE]] evaluation that was also mostly positive and found Palantir software to be interoperable with other systems such as [[Distributed Common Ground System|DCGS-A]]. The Pentagon subsequently presented Congress with reports from the Army's acquisition office that contradicted these findings. ''[[Fortune (magazine)|Fortune]]'' notes that the mutual distrust came from differences in working cultures and that Palantir approached the Pentagon with the attitude that the Pentagon was incompetent in developing data platforms.<ref name= "BrillFortune"/> In 2016, Palantir won a lawsuit against the Army for discriminating against its commercial solution in favor of DCGS-A, which was more expensive and risk-prone. The Army appealed in 2017, but the matter was settled after Palantir defeated Raytheon in a head-to-head competition and Palantir won the contract in 2019.<ref>{{cite news |last1= Judson |first1= Jen |title= Palantir — who successfully sued the Army — has won a major Army contract |url= https://www.defensenews.com/land/2019/03/29/palantir-who-successfully-sued-the-army-just-won-a-major-army-contract/ |work= Defense News |date= 1 April 2019 |language= en}}</ref>

===Algorithm development===
[[I2 Group|i2 Inc]] sued Palantir in Federal Court, alleging fraud, conspiracy, and copyright infringement over Palantir's algorithm. Shyam Sankar, Palantir's then-director of business development, used a [[Private investigator|private eye]] company known as the [[Cutout (espionage)|cutout]] for obtaining i2's code. i2 settled out of court for $10 million in 2011.<ref name=Bloomberg180419Waldman/>

===WikiLeaks proposals (2010)===
In 2010, [[Hunton & Williams]] LLP allegedly asked Berico Technologies, Palantir, and [[HBGary Federal]] to draft a response plan to "the [[WikiLeaks]] Threat". In early 2011 [[Anonymous (group)|Anonymous]] publicly released HBGary-internal documents, including the plan. The plan proposed that Palantir software would "serve as the foundation for all the data collection, integration, analysis, and production efforts".<ref name="Harris">{{Cite web |last=Harris |first=Shane |date=January 31, 2012 |title=Killer App |url=https://washingtonian.com/2012/01/31/killer-app/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133450/https://www.washingtonian.com/2012/01/31/killer-app/ |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |access-date=March 14, 2012 |website=Washingtonian}}</ref> The plan also included slides, allegedly authored by HBGary CEO Aaron Barr, which suggested "[spreading] disinformation" and "disrupting" [[Glenn Greenwald]]'s support for WikiLeaks.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Wray |first1=James |last2=Stabe |first2=Ulf |title=Data intelligence firms proposed a systematic attack against WikiLeaks |url=http://www.thetechherald.com/articles/Data-intelligence-firms-proposed-a-systematic-attack-against-WikiLeaks/12751/ |url-status=usurped |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181126010658/http://www.thetechherald.com/articles/Data-intelligence-firms-proposed-a-systematic-attack-against-WikiLeaks/12751/ |archive-date=November 26, 2018 |access-date=January 30, 2012 |website=The Tech Herald}}</ref> Palantir CEO [[Alex Karp]] ended all ties to [[HBGary]] and issued a statement apologizing to "progressive organizations ... and Greenwald ... for any involvement that we may have had in these matters". Palantir placed an employee on leave pending a review by a third-party law firm. The employee was later reinstated.<ref name="Harris" />

===Racial discrimination lawsuit (2016)===
On September 26, 2016, the [[Office of Federal Contract Compliance Programs]] of the [[United States Department of Labor|U.S. Department of Labor]] filed a lawsuit against Palantir alleging that the company discriminated against Asian job applicants on the basis of their race.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Complaint for violations of Executive Order 11246 |url=https://www.dol.gov/sites/default/files/newsroom/newsreleases/OFCCP20160926_0.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161001163715/https://www.dol.gov/sites/default/files/newsroom/newsreleases/OFCCP20160926_0.pdf |archive-date=October 1, 2016 |access-date=September 27, 2016 |publisher=[[United States Department of Justice]]}}</ref> According to the lawsuit, the company "routinely eliminated" Asian applicants during the hiring process, even when they were "as qualified as white applicants" for the same jobs.<ref name="vanityfair">{{Cite magazine |last=Kosoff |first=Maya |date=September 27, 2016 |title=U.S. Government sues Peter Thiel's secretive big-data start-up for discrimination |url=http://www.vanityfair.com/news/2016/09/us-government-sues-palantir-discrimination |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161104153529/http://www.vanityfair.com/news/2016/09/us-government-sues-palantir-discrimination |archive-date=November 4, 2016 |access-date=October 31, 2016 |magazine=Vanity Fair}}</ref> Palantir settled the suit in April 2017 for $1.7 million while not admitting wrongdoing.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Kravets |first=David |date=April 26, 2017 |title=Palantir settles US charges that it discriminated against Asian engineers |website=Ars Technica |url=https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2017/04/palantir-settles-us-charges-it-discriminated-against-asian-engineers/ |url-status=live |access-date=April 26, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133450/https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2017/04/palantir-settles-us-charges-it-discriminated-against-asian-engineers/ |archive-date=October 28, 2020}}</ref>

===UK===
During questioning in front of the [[Digital, Culture, Media and Sport Committee|Digital, Culture, Media and Sport Select Committee]], [[Christopher Wylie]], the former research director of [[Cambridge Analytica]], said that Palantir and Cambridge Analytica had had several meetings and that [[SCL Group|SCL]] chief executive Alexander Nix had facilitated its use of [[Aleksandr Kogan (scientist)|Aleksandr Kogan]]'s data, which had been obtained from his app "thisisyourdigitallife" by mining personal surveys. Kogan later established Global Science Research to share the data with Cambridge Analytica and others. Wylie confirmed that employees from both Cambridge Analytica and Palantir used Kogan's Global Science Research and harvested Facebook data together in the same offices.<ref name="Guardian180327Pegg">{{Cite news |last1=Pegg |first1=David |last2=Cadwalladr |first2=Carole |author-link2=Carole Cadwalladr |date=March 27, 2018 |title=US data firm admits employee approached Cambridge Analytica: Palantir confirm employee 'engaged in a personal capacity' with the company |work=The Guardian |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2018/mar/28/palantir-employee-cambridge-analytica |url-status=live |access-date=April 23, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133402/https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2018/mar/28/palantir-employee-cambridge-analytica |archive-date=October 28, 2020}}</ref><ref name="NYT180327">{{Cite news |last1=Confessore |first1=Nicholas |author-link=Nicholas Confessore |last2=Rosenberg |first2=Matthew |author-link2=Matthew Rosenberg |date=March 27, 2018 |title=Spy Contractor's Idea Helped Cambridge Analytica Harvest Facebook Data |work=[[The New York Times]] |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/03/27/us/cambridge-analytica-palantir.html |url-status=live |access-date=April 24, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133423/https://www.nytimes.com/2018/03/27/us/cambridge-analytica-palantir.html |archive-date=October 28, 2020}}</ref> Palantir said it had contact with Cambridge Analytica but decided not to move forward with a relationship. Palantir also said that it discovered only in March 2018 that one of its employees, Alfredas Chmieliauskas, had worked with Cambridge Analytica in a personal capacity.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pegg |first1=David |last2=Cadwalladr |first2=Carole |title=US data firm admits employee approached Cambridge Analytica |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2018/mar/28/palantir-employee-cambridge-analytica |work=The Guardian |date=March 28, 2018}}</ref> When shown proof of Chmieliauskas's activities, the company retracted its denial and issued a press release saying that Chmieliauskas had "engaged in an entirely personal capacity". 

Testifying before British lawmakers the same day  ''The Times'' published its story about Palantir and CA, Wylie said that "senior Palantir employees" had also been involved with CA. He said that "Palantir staff would come into the [CA] office and work on the data". In an interview with ''The Times'', Wylie said that he and Nix had visited Palantir's London office.<ref>The Philosopher In The Valley Alex Karp, Palantir, and the Rise of the Surveillance State by Michael Steinberger Avid Reader Press (2025)</ref> ''The New York Times'' wrote, "emails reviewed by ''The Times'' indicate that Mr. Nix and Mr. Chmieliauskas sought to revive talks about a formal partnership through early 2014, but Palantir executives again declined." Wylie admitted that Palantir and Cambridge Analytica signed no contract and had no formal relationship. ''The New York Times'' also wrote that Wylie did not identify or specify the number of Palantir employees that he mentioned.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Confessore |first1=Nicholas |last2=Rosenberg |first2=Matthew |title=Spy Contractor’s Idea Helped Cambridge Analytica Harvest Facebook Data (Published 2018) |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/03/27/us/cambridge-analytica-palantir.html |date=March 28, 2018 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20251208215906/https://www.nytimes.com/2018/03/27/us/cambridge-analytica-palantir.html |archive-date=December 8, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> In June 2021, reacting to the NHS's use of Foundry, the tech-justice nonprofit Foxglove began a campaign against Palantir because "Their background has generally been in contracts where people are harmed, not healed." Supporting the campaign, [[Clive Lewis MP]] said Palantir had an "appalling track record".<ref name="CNBC">{{cite news |title=Campaign launches to try to force Palantir out of Britain's NHS |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2021/06/04/campaign-launched-to-get-palantir-out-of-nhs.html |access-date=June 16, 2021 |publisher=CNBC |date=June 4, 2021}}</ref> Palantir UK is headed by [[Louis Mosley]], grandson of British fascist leader [[Oswald Mosley]].

Palantir UK came under scrutiny after allegations about former British Labour politician and Ambassador to the United States [[Peter Mandelson]]. In 2026, The Chartered Institute of Public Relations (CIPR) called for the immediate release of fully transparent information relating to Mandelson's lobbying firm, Global Counsel, and its role in securing government contracts for Palantir, warning that the current lobbying system is "failing the public".<ref>{{cite news |title=Mandelson's links with US tech firm Palantir must be fully exposed, campaigners warn |url=https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2026/feb/04/peter-mandelson-palantir-jeffrey-epstein-government |access-date=April 29, 2026 |date=February 4, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=CIPR calls for transparency over Mandelson lobbying links |url=https://newsroom.cipr.co.uk/cipr-calls-for-transparency-over-mandelson-lobbying-links/ |access-date=April 29, 2026 |date=February 5, 2026}}</ref>
===ICE partnership (since 2014)===
Palantir has come under criticism due to its partnership developing software for [[U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement]] (ICE). In a statement provided to ''[[The New York Times]]'',<ref>{{Cite web |date=December 11, 2018 |title=DealBook Briefing: Investors May Be Overlooking Their Own Discrimination |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/11/business/dealbook/investor-bias-discrimination.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190804201053/https://www.nytimes.com/2018/12/11/business/dealbook/investor-bias-discrimination.html |archive-date=August 4, 2019 |access-date=August 4, 2019 |website=The New York Times}}</ref> the firm implied that because its contract was with HSI, a division of ICE focused on investigating criminal activities, it played no role in deportations. In 2017, ''[[The Intercept]]'' obtained documents that disprove this claim and show that ICE considers Palantir's ICM software "mission critical".<ref name="DeportationMachine">{{Cite web |last=Woodman |first=Spencer |date=March 2, 2017 |title=Palantir Provides the Engine for Donald Trump's Deportation Machine |url=https://theintercept.com/2017/03/02/palantir-provides-the-engine-for-donald-trumps-deportation-machine/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133357/https://theintercept.com/2017/03/02/palantir-provides-the-engine-for-donald-trumps-deportation-machine/ |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |access-date=November 6, 2018 |website=The Intercept}}</ref> The documents obtained by ''The Intercept'' and Mijente, an immigration advocacy group, show that in 2017 HSI and ERO undertook a joint operation using the ICM database to arrest and possibly deport family members of undocumented children caught trying to cross the border. In August 2019, when HSI, deploying FALCON, led a raid on food-processing plants in Mississippi, nearly 700 people were arrested. In an interview with CNBC a few months later, Karp acknowledged that Palantir's blanket denials had been misleading.<ref>The Philosopher In The Valley Alex Karp, Palantir, and the Rise of the Surveillance State by Michael Steinberger Avid Reader Press (2025) pps. 155-164</ref> But in 2019, he renewed Palantir's contract with ICE for $50 million over three years.<ref>The Philosopher In The Valley Alex Karp, Palantir, and the Rise of the Surveillance State by Michael Steinberger Avid Reader Press (2025) pps. 155-164</ref> Other groups critical of Palantir include the [[Brennan Center for Justice]],<ref>{{Cite web |title=Palantir Contract Dispute Exposes NYPD's Lack of Transparency |url=https://www.brennancenter.org/blog/palantir-contract-dispute-exposes-nypd%E2%80%99s-lack-transparency |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133409/https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/analysis-opinion/palantir-contract-dispute-exposes-nypds-lack-transparency |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |access-date=August 4, 2019 |publisher=Brennan Center for Justice}}</ref> National Immigration Project,<ref>{{Cite web |title=National Immigration Project of the National Lawyers Guild |url=https://www.nationalimmigrationproject.org/index.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133434/https://www.nationalimmigrationproject.org/index.html |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |publisher=National Immigration Project |access-date=August 4, 2019}}</ref> the Immigrant Defense Project,<ref>{{Cite web |title=Immigrant Defense Project - Fighting for Justice & Human Rights for ALL |url=https://www.immigrantdefenseproject.org/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133408/https://www.immigrantdefenseproject.org/ |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |access-date=August 4, 2019}}</ref> the [[Tech Workers Coalition]], and [[Mijente]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Mijente |url=https://mijente.net/notechforice/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190804200729/https://mijente.net/notechforice/ |archive-date=August 4, 2019 |access-date=August 4, 2019 |website=Mijente}}</ref> In one internal ICE report Mijente acquired, Palantir's software was said to have been critical in an operation to arrest the parents of children residing illegally.<ref>{{Cite web |date=May 2, 2019 |title=Palantir Played Key Role in Arresting Families for Deportation, Document Shows |url=https://mijente.net/2019/05/02/palantir-arresting-families/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133553/https://mijente.net/2019/05/palantir-arresting-families/ |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |access-date=August 4, 2019 |website=Mijente}}</ref> In September 2020, [[Amnesty International]] released a report criticizing Palantir's failure to conduct [[human rights due diligence]] for its contracts with ICE. Palantir's human rights record has been scrutinized for contributing to [[human rights violations]] of [[asylum-seekers]] and [[Economic migrant|migrants]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Failing To Do Right:The Urgent Need for Palantir to Respect Human Rights |url=https://www.amnestyusa.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/Amnest-International-Palantir-Briefing-Report-092520_Final.pdf |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133415/https://www.amnestyusa.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/Amnest-International-Palantir-Briefing-Report-092520_Final.pdf |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |access-date=September 28, 2020 |publisher=Amnesty International}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Amnesty International slams Palantir's human rights record |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/palantir-human-rights-record-amnesty-international/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028133416/https://www.cbsnews.com/news/palantir-human-rights-record-amnesty-international/ |archive-date=October 28, 2020 |access-date=September 29, 2020 |publisher=CBS News|date=September 29, 2020}}</ref> In April 2025, Palantir signed a new $30 million contract with ICE to provide software for a new platform called ImmigrationOS, which would expedite deportations.<ref>The Philosopher In The Valley Alex Karp, Palantir, and the Rise of the Surveillance State by Michael Steinberger Avid Reader Press (2025) p. 282</ref>

====Immigration Lifecycle Operating System (Immigration OS)====
In April 2025, Palantir was reported to be working closely with ICE to enable [[deportation in the second presidency of Donald Trump]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Cox · |first=Joseph |date=April 17, 2025 |title=Leaked: Palantir's Plan to Help ICE Deport People |url=https://www.404media.co/leaked-palantirs-plan-to-help-ice-deport-people/ |access-date=April 17, 2025 |website=404 Media |language=en}}</ref> ICE reportedly paid Palantir $30 million to develop the Immigration Lifecycle Operating System (Immigration OS).<ref>{{cite web |last1=Haskins |first1=Caroline |title=ICE is Paying Palantir $30 Million to Build 'ImmigrationOS' Surveillance Program |url=https://www.wired.com/story/ice-palantir-immigrationos/ |website=Wired |date=April 18, 2025}}</ref> ''[[The Washington Post]]'' reported that Palantir developed the software to track undocumented immigrants with the goal of deporting them faster.<ref name="WashingtonPostICE"/> The Immigration OS project was renewed in September 2025, despite facing some Palantir employees questioning whether "the contract should be discontinued if ICE's use of the technology veers into extrajudicial actions or violate the company's civil liberties principles".<ref name="WashingtonPostICE">{{cite web |last1=Dou |first1=Eva |title=How Palantir shifted course to play key role in ICE deportations |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2025/12/03/palantir-immigration-ice/ |website=The Washington Post |date=December 3, 2025}}</ref> In an October 2025 interview with ''The New York Times'', Palantir chief technology officer Shyam Sankar said that Immigration OS "tracked encounters at the border, asylum applications and applications for benefits". According to Palantir, Immigration OS "does not track information of U.S. citizens who are relatives of undocumented immigrants".<ref name="WashingtonPostICE"/><ref>{{cite web |last1=Douthat |first1=Ross |last2=Boyd |first2=Sophia |title=What Palantir Sees |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/10/30/opinion/palantir-shyam-sankar-military.html |website=The New York Times |date=October 30, 2025}}</ref>

The Immigration OS project has also been criticized by [[Y Combinator]] co-founder [[Paul Graham (programmer)|Paul Graham]], who wrote that Palantir is "building the infrastructure of the police state".<ref name="WashingtonPostICE"/> Palantir's continued affiliation with ICE coincided with the changes to its employee code of conduct that were made in response to executive orders by President Trump forbidding federal contractors from diversity practices.<ref name="WashingtonPostICE"/> In 2022, [[DHS]] awarded Palantir $139.3 million for "investigative case management operations, maintenance support services and custom enhancements"; the contract is ongoing {{As of|2026|January|lc=y}}.<ref>{{Cite web|author=USA Government|title=Contract Summary, Delivery Order (DO) PIID 70CTD022FR0000170 |url=https://www.usaspending.gov/award/CONT_AWD_70CTD022FR0000170_7012_GS35F0086U_4730|access-date=January 26, 2026 |website=USAspending.gov|year=2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|author=Ty Roush|title=These Companies—Palantir, AT&T, Deloitte—Have The Biggest ICE Contracts As DHS Funding Under Fire|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/tylerroush/2026/01/26/these-companies-palantir-att-deloitte-have-the-biggest-ice-contracts-as-dhs-funding-under-fire/|access-date=January 26, 2026 |website=Forbes |date=January 26, 2026}}</ref>

==="HHS Protect Now" and privacy concerns (2020)===
The [[COVID-19 pandemic]] prompted tech companies to respond to growing demand for citizen information from governments in order to conduct contact tracing and to analyze patient data.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Muhlberg |first1=Byron |title=Privacy Advocates Calls for Transparency Over Use of Patient Data |url=https://www.cpomagazine.com/data-privacy/privacy-advocates-calls-for-transparency-over-use-of-patient-data/ |website=CPO Magazine |date=May 13, 2020 |access-date=November 30, 2020 |archive-date=October 24, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201024091633/https://www.cpomagazine.com/data-privacy/privacy-advocates-calls-for-transparency-over-use-of-patient-data/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Consequently, data collection companies such as Palantir had been contracted to partake in pandemic data collection practices. Palantir's participation in "HHS Protect Now", a program launched by the United States Department of Health and Human Services to track the spread of the coronavirus, attracted criticism from US lawmakers.<ref name="Albergotti">{{cite news |last1=Albergotti |first1=Reed |title=Lawmakers call for more transparency in health agency's pandemic data collection practices |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2020/07/01/warren-hhs-data-collection/ |newspaper=The Washington Post |date=July 1, 2020 |access-date=November 30, 2020 |archive-date=December 24, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201224194018/https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2020/07/01/warren-hhs-data-collection/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

Palantir's participation in COVID-19 response projects reignited debates over its involvement in tracking illegal immigrants, especially its alleged effects on digital inequality and restrictions on online freedoms. Critics allege that confidential data acquired by HHS could be exploited by other federal agencies in unregulated and potentially harmful ways.<ref name=Albergotti /> Alternative proposals request greater transparency in the process to determine whether any of the data aggregated would be shared with the US Immigration and Customs Enforcement to single out illegal immigrants.<ref name=Albergotti />

=== Second Trump administration (2025)===
According to required financial disclosures, [[Stephen Miller]], who as [[Homeland Security Advisor|United States homeland security advisor]] has been actively involved in the [[second Trump administration]]'s deportation efforts, owns between $100,000 and $250,000 of Palantir stock; some Trump critics have raised concerns about a potential conflict of interest.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Trump Adviser Stephen Miller Holds Six-Figure Investment in Tech Firm Profiting from Deportations |url=https://www.democracynow.org/2025/6/25/headlines/trump_adviser_stephen_miller_holds_six_figure_investment_in_tech_firm_profiting_from_deportations |access-date=September 16, 2025 |website=Democracy Now! |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |title=Damning Report Exposes Stephen Miller's Shady Ties to Palantir |url=https://newrepublic.com/post/197149/stephen-miller-palantir-stocks-immigration-report |access-date=June 24, 2025 |magazine=The New Republic |issn=0028-6583}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Stephen Miller's Financial Stake in ICE Contractor Palantir |url=https://www.pogo.org/investigations/stephen-miller-conflicts-of-interest |access-date=September 16, 2025 |website=POGO |language=en-US}}</ref> Similar financial disclosure requirements of U.S. government employees show that at least 10 other members of the Trump administration own Palantir stock.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Stephen Miller's Financial Stake in ICE Contractor Palantir |url=https://www.pogo.org/investigations/stephen-miller-conflicts-of-interest |access-date=June 24, 2025 |website=POGO |language=en-US}}</ref> Advocacy groups such as the [[Alliance for Secure AI]] have also criticized Palantir's use of AI during the second Trump administration.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hornberger |first=Peyton |title=We're creating AI that could surveil US citizens. And the government is in on it. |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/opinion/2025/07/25/ai-surveillance-palantir-openai-data/85341278007/ |access-date=September 16, 2025 |website=USA Today |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Osborne |first1=James |last2=Chronicle |first2=Houston |date=August 12, 2025 |title=Ted Cruz's Campaign to Keep Government Out of AI |url=https://insider.govtech.com/texas/news/ted-cruzs-campaign-to-keep-government-out-of-ai |access-date=September 16, 2025 |website=Industry Insider Texas |language=en}}</ref>

In February 2025, ''The New York Times'' reported that Trump and Palantir were compiling a master list of personal information on every American that could give Trump "untold surveillance power" by illegally combining information from various government departments.<ref>DOGE access to US intelligence secrets poses a national security threat, Democrats say | AP News</ref><ref>Trump, DOGE Ignore Privacy Laws to Gather Americans' Personal Info</ref><ref>The Verge: House Democrats: DOGE is building a 'master database' of Americans' sensitive information – EPIC – Electronic Privacy Information Center</ref><ref> Tech Newsday DOGE Accused of Creating Massive Surveillance System Under Musk-Led Trump Administration
</ref><ref>Opinion | Elon Musk's Legacy: DOGE's Construction of a Surveillance State - The New York Times)</ref><ref>Real mission of DOGE seen in new insights on federal data and privacy</ref> Palantir denies this.{{citation needed|date=June 2026}} 
{{Immigration policy of the second Trump administration sidebar | expanded=technology}}

In December 2025, it was reported that Palantir had begun to develop a portal for the [[United States Department of Education|Department of Education]] allowing universities to report foreign donations. The company is acting as a subcontractor of Monkton, a computer and network security company, which won a $9.8 million contract to design, develop, and deploy a "[[Higher Education Act of 1965|Section 117 Information Sharing Environment Capable of Providing Greater Transparency]]".<ref>{{cite web |last1=Nazzaro |first1=Miranda |title=Palantir quietly lands in Education Department through foreign funding portal |url=https://fedscoop.com/palantir-education-department-foreign-funding-influence-portal/ |publisher=FedScoop |date=December 4, 2025}}</ref>

===German police software (2020s)===
There is considerable controversy about the use of Palantir by the state and the Federal Ministry of Interior's ongoing attempt to introduce it at the federal level. The controversy also elicits debate from neighboring countries, with ''[[De Tijd]]'' noting that even when a German court ruled the software unconstitutional in 2023, it was not abandoned, and the law was changed.<ref>{{cite news |title=Palantir, bedrijf zonder scrupules: "Soms doden we onze vijanden" |url=https://www.standaard.be/buitenland/palantir-bedrijf-zonder-scrupules-soms-doden-we-onze-vijanden/86971093.html |work=De Standaard |date=August 29, 2025 |language=nl-BE}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Palantir, de AI-lieveling van beleggers en de datareus achter Trumps deportaties |url=https://www.tijd.be/politiek-economie/internationaal/vs/palantir-de-ai-lieveling-van-beleggers-en-de-datareus-achter-trumps-deportaties/10619193.html |access-date=September 3, 2025 |work=De Tijd |date=August 9, 2025 |language=nl}}</ref><ref name="euractiv1">{{cite news |last1=Pugnet |first1=Aurélie |last2=Henning |first2=Maximilian |last3=Strohmaier |first3=Brenda |title=Palantir is well on its way to conquering Europe |url=https://www.euractiv.com/section/tech/news/palantir-is-well-on-its-way-to-conquering-europe/ |access-date=September 3, 2025 |date=August 8, 2025}}</ref>

The controversy also touches on Thiel's and Karp's German origins and alleged relationships with powerful political and economic actors in Germany (especially in the wealthier, more powerful states, such as [[Bavaria]], [[Baden-Württemberg]], [[North Rhine-Westphalia]], and [[Hesse]]).<ref>{{cite news |last1=Goebel |first1=Jacqueline |title=WirtschaftsWoche |url=https://www.wiwo.de/unternehmen/it/umstrittener-boersengang-die-deutschland-connection-von-palantir/26129678.html |work=wiwo.de |date=August 26, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite episode|last1=Hagen |first1=Lisa Maria|last2=Jeric |first2=Lorenz |date=August 19, 2025|title=Polizei-Software: Können wir Palantir ersetzen |series=Polizei-Software: Können wir Palantir ersetzen|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JfvwI1AX5aQ|location=Germany|publisher= [[Funk (service)|Funk (ZDF)]]}}</ref> Hesse is Thiel's home state (he was born in Frankfurt), and Karp completed his PhD there.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lindner |first1=Roland |title=Neu an der Börse: Die Exzentriker von Palantir |url=https://www.faz.net/aktuell/wirtschaft/neu-an-der-boerse-die-exzentriker-von-palantir-16964566.html |work=Faz.net |date=September 22, 2020 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20240218050203/https://www.faz.net/aktuell/wirtschaft/neu-an-der-boerse-die-exzentriker-von-palantir-16964566.html |archive-date=February 18, 2024 |language=de}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Palantir in Hessen - vereint Daten von Facebook & Co mit polizeilichen Datenbanken?? |url=https://police-it.net/hessendata-auftragserteilung-systembetrieb |work=Police-IT |date=November 2, 2018 |language=de}}</ref> Hesse was the first federal state to introduce Palantir software (as Hessendata, since 2017<ref>{{cite news |title=Palantir in Deutschland - Datenschützer warnen vor Polizei-Software aus den USA |url=https://www.deutschlandfunk.de/palantir-deutschland-polizei-software-datenschutz-100.html |work=Deutschlandfunk |date=June 5, 2025 |language=de}}</ref>) while Bavaria promotes its VeRA version at the federal level. Bavaria Minister of the Interior [[Joachim Herrmann (CSU)|Joachim Herrmann]] has said that VeRA must be used nationwide to "create a uniform and effective security infrastructure".<ref>{{cite news |title=Herrmann: VeRA ist sinnvoll und notwendig – Bayerisches Landesportal |url=https://www.bayern.de/herrmann-vera-ist-sinnvoll-und-notwendig/ |date=April 21, 2025}}</ref>

There is also debate about the founders' application of the philosophy of [[René Girard]], [[Friedrich Nietzsche|Nietzsche]], [[Carl Schmitt]], and the [[Frankfurt School]] at Palantir and in the surveillance industry.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Schuppisser |first1=Raffael |title=Ich bin dynamit war Friedrich Nietzsche der wegbereiter fuer Trump |url=https://www.bzbasel.ch/kultur/analyse-ich-bin-dynamit-war-friedrich-nietzsche-der-wegbereiter-fuer-trump-ld.2807908 |date=August 24, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Schütt |first1=Julian |title=Dieser Philosoph hilft Trump, den perfekten Überwachungsstaat zu schaffen |url=https://www.watson.ch/international/usa/332665371-philosoph-alex-karp-hilft-trump-den-ueberwachungsstaat-zu-schaffen |work=[[Watson (newspaper)|Watson]] |date=August 10, 2025 |language=de}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Reuter |first1=Markus |title=Palantir: Wer jetzt bei Peter Thiel Software kauft, hat wirklich nichts verstanden |url=https://netzpolitik.org/2025/palantir-wer-jetzt-bei-peter-thiel-software-kauft-hat-wirklich-nichts-verstanden/ |work=netzpolitik.org |date=March 24, 2025 |language=de}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Fischer |first1=Stefan |title=Podcast "Die Peter Thiel Story" im Deutschlandfunk: Die dunkle Aufklärung |url=https://www.sueddeutsche.de/medien/podcast-peter-thiel-deutschlandfunk-trump-usa-li.3258008 |work=Süddeutsche Zeitung |date=May 28, 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250606103121/https://www.sueddeutsche.de/medien/podcast-peter-thiel-deutschlandfunk-trump-usa-li.3258008 |archive-date=June 6, 2025 |language=de}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Mangold |first1=Ijoma |title=Peter Thiel: Eine Milliarde Ideen |url=https://www.zeit.de/kultur/2025-07/peter-thiel-paypal-palantir-unternehmen-tech-milliardaer |work=Die Zeit |date=July 30, 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20250811104357/https://www.zeit.de/kultur/2025-07/peter-thiel-paypal-palantir-unternehmen-tech-milliardaer |archive-date=August 11, 2025 |language=de-DE}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Mayer |first1=Alf |title=Watching You – Die Welt von Palantir und Alex Karp |url=https://culturmag.de/crimemag/alf-mayer-sieht-watching-you-die-welt-von-palantir-und-alex-karp/163259 |date=June 1, 2024}}</ref> According to a 2025 documentary produced by ''[[Hessischer Rundfunk]]'', Karp, like Thiel, seeks world domination.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lohr |first1=Matthias |title=Kasseler Filmemacher über Palantir: Tech-Milliardäre "wollen die Weltherrschaft" |url=https://www.hna.de/kassel/die-weltherrschaft-filmemacher-ueber-palantir-tech-milliardaere-wollen-93866748.html |work=hna.de |date=August 7, 2025 |language=de}}</ref> Franz-Josef Hanke, a spokesperson for the [[Humanist Union]] in Hesse, has also said that this is Thiel's goal with Palantir.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanke |first1=Franz-Josef |title=Keine Geschäfte mit Palantir und Peter Thiel! |url=https://www.humanistische-union.de/publikationen/mitteilungen/mitteilungen-254/publikation/keine-geschaefte-mit-palantir-und-peter-thiel/ |work=Humanistische Union |date=April 22, 2025 |language=de}}</ref>

The German version of ''[[Jacobin (magazine)|Jacobin]]'' has written that while Palantir does not steal data or carry out surveillance activity, its increased power in Germany is still worrisome, considering the founders' non-democratic tendencies, and that [[Social Democratic Party of Germany|SPD]] and [[Alliance 90/The Greens]] also carry responsibility.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Kullick |first1=Niklas |title=Palantir steht für einen digitalen Rechtsruck |url=https://jacobin.de/artikel/palantir-dobrindt-polizei-ueberwachung |work=Jacobin Magazin |date=August 23, 2025 |language=de}}</ref> ''[[The European (2009 magazine)|The European]]'' has written that Thiel and Karp cause themselves trouble by trying to create a mysterious aura, which leads to a negative reaction to Palantir software despite its usefulness in civilian and defense applications.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Stock |first1=Oliver |title=SPD lehnt Einsatz einer US-Software für Verbrechensbekämpfung ab |url=https://www.theeuropean.de/politik/spd-lehnt-einsatz-einer-us-software-fuer-verbrechensbekaempfung-ab |work=The European |date=June 13, 2025 |language=de-DE}}</ref>

According to an August 2025 ''[[Süddeutsche Zeitung]]'' article, a [[YouGov]] poll on behalf of the ''SZ Dossier'' found that a slim majority of Germans support federal agencies' use of Palantir: 51% of respondents partly or completely supported it, 30% partly or completely opposed it, and 19% did not give a response.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Rinaldi |first1=Gabriel |title=Was die Deutschen über den Einsatz von Palantir-Software denken (Meldung) |url=https://www.sz-dossier.de/meldungen/was-die-deutschen-ueber-den-einsatz-von-palantir-software-denken-ba13cf73 |work=SZ Dossier |date=August 20, 2025 |language=de}}</ref> Nevertheless, civil skepticism of the company and software remains prominent; a petition on the platform ''Campact'' gathered 84,000 signatures against Palantir within a few hours and has more than 400,000 as of September 2025.<ref>{{cite web |title=Nein zum Palantir-Deal – jetzt Appell unterzeichnen |url=https://aktion.campact.de/datenschutz/peter-thiel-palantir-polizei-software-stoppen-petition/teilnehmen |website=aktion.campact.de |language=de}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Big Data bei der Polizei: (Nicht nur) Hessen sucht mit Palantir-Software (nicht nur) nach Gefährdern |url=https://www.labournet.de/interventionen/grundrechte/grundrechte-all/polizeistaat/big-data-bei-der-polizei-hessen-sucht-mit-palantir-software-nach-gefaehrdern/ |access-date=September 3, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Joswig |first1=Gareth |title=Klage gegen KI-Überwachungs-Software in Bayern |url=https://taz.de/Verfassungsbeschwerde-gegen-Palantir-/!6102522/ |work=Die Tageszeitung: taz |date=July 23, 2025 |language=de}}</ref>

=== Corporate tax practices ===
An [[Institute on Taxation and Economic Policy]] (ITEP) report found that Palantir Technologies paid no [[Income tax in the United States|U.S. federal income tax]] in 2025 despite reporting about $1.5 billion in domestic income. According to the analysis, the company's zero tax liability was largely due to provisions in [[One Big Beautiful Bill Act|recent U.S. tax legislation]], particularly rules allowing immediate deduction of [[research and development]] expenses, which significantly reduced its taxable income. The report also noted that Palantir has benefited from substantial [[Government procurement|government contracts]], including work with U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE), while simultaneously receiving tax advantages through the [[Taxation in the United States|federal tax code]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Palantir Pays Zero Federal Income Tax Despite Explosive Growth, Largely Due to Trump Tax Law |url=https://itep.org/palantir-pays-zero-tax-trump-tax-law-ice/ |access-date=2026-03-22 |website=ITEP |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== AI-assisted targeting tools ===
In April 2026, Palantir published a manifesto based on a book co-authored by Alex Karp that frames AI weapons proliferation using Cold War-style [[Deterrence theory|deterrence logic]], warning that adversaries "will proceed"<ref name=":8">{{Cite web |date=2026-04-19 |title=Palantir posted a manifesto that reads like the ramblings of a comic book villain |url=https://www.engadget.com/big-tech/palantir-posted-a-manifesto-that-reads-like-the-ramblings-of-a-comic-book-villain-181947361.html |access-date=2026-04-22 |website=Engadget |language=en-US}}</ref> regardless of ethical debate, which has faced backlash for framing AI weapons development as inevitable and necessary for [[US hegemony]].<ref name=":8" /><ref>{{Cite news |title=Palantir's "Progressive" Manifesto: an AI Death Drive? {{!}} Steven Methven |url=https://novaramedia.com/2026/04/20/palantirs-progressive-manifesto-an-ai-death-drive/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260422065432/https://novaramedia.com/2026/04/20/palantirs-progressive-manifesto-an-ai-death-drive/ |archive-date=2026-04-22 |access-date=2026-04-22 |work=Novara Media |language=en-GB}}</ref> The manifesto's claim that "No other country in the history of the world has advanced progressive values more than this one"<ref name=":8" /> has been criticized by academics and analysts for Western [[supremacism]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Staff |first=Al Jazeera |title="Technofascism": Critics accuse Palantir of pushing AI war doctrine |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/20/technofascism-critics-accuse-palantir-of-pushing-ai-war-doctrine |access-date=2026-04-22 |website=Al Jazeera |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":8" />

In June 2025, [[Francesca Albanese]]'s report for the United Nations on [[List of companies involved in the Gaza war|corporations complicit]] in the [[Gaza genocide]] named Palantir as a source of AI tools linked to the Israeli military's activities in Gaza.<ref>{{cite news |last=Borger |first=Julian |date=July 3, 2025 |title=Global firms 'profiting from genocide' in Gaza, says UN rapporteur|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/jul/03/global-firms-profiting-israel-genocide-gaza-united-nations-rapporteur|work=[[The Guardian]] |location= |publisher= |access-date=April 23, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Marsi|first=Federica|date=July 1, 2025 |title=UN report lists companies complicit in Israel's "genocide": Who are they?|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/7/1/un-report-lists-companies-complicit-in-israels-genocide-who-are-they|work=[[Al Jazeera]] |location= |publisher= |access-date=April 23, 2026}}</ref>

