For Q4 2025, Palantir reported revenue of $1.4 billion, a 70% increase year-over-year, driven largely by rapid enterprise adoption of its AI Platform (AIP). U.S. commercial revenue rose 137% year-over-year to $507 million. Total contract value reached a record $4.3 billion in the quarter. For 2026, management guided revenue of approximately $2.2 billion, implying 61% growth.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Palantir Q4 2025 Earnings |url=https://investors.palantir.com/news |website=Palantir Investor Relations |access-date=2026-03-21}}</ref>

In December 2025, the UK [[Ministry of Defence (United Kingdom)|Ministry of Defence]] awarded Palantir a £240 million three-year contract.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Palantir wins £240m MoD contract |url=https://finance.yahoo.com/news/3-reasons-palantir-unavoidable-ai-193400121.html |website=Yahoo Finance |access-date=2026-03-21}}</ref>

==Influence on other companies==
Palantir's success has led a new wave of companies (mostly in the defense sector) to follow its business model, often connected to its personnel. ''[[The Wall Street Journal]]'' and ''[[Business Insider]]'' have described a "Palantir Mafia" that they compared to the [[PayPal Mafia]]. ''The Wall Street Journal'' has written that venture-capital firms, such as Palumni VC and XYZ Capital, have emerged with a mission to invest in startups founded by former Palantir employees.<ref name="wsjmafia"/> Those mentioned include founders of companies like [[Anduril Industries]], [[8VC]], [[Addepar]], and [[Ironclad (software)|Ironclad]].<ref>{{cite news |first1=Samantha |last1=Stokes |first2=Julia |last2=Hornstein |first3=Melia |last3=Russell |title=Meet the new tech mafia in town: the Palantir Mafia |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/palantir-mafia-former-employees-startups-anduril-2025-3#trae-stephens-matt-grimm-and-brian-schimpf-anduril-2 |access-date=August 14, 2025 |work=Business Insider}}</ref><ref name="wsjmafia">{{Cite news |last=Au-Yeung |first=Angel |date=August 15, 2025 |title=The Palantir Mafia Behind Silicon Valley's Hottest Startups |url=https://www.wsj.com/tech/the-palantir-mafia-behind-silicon-valleys-hottest-startups-f6e9bcbb |access-date=August 17, 2025 |work=The Wall Street Journal |language=en-US}}</ref> Joe Lonsdale also co-founded anti-drone tech startup Epirus.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lonsdale |first1=Joe |title=The American Optimist: An Interview with Joe Lonsdale |url=https://profectusmag.com/the-american-optimist-joe-lonsdale-talks-with-archbridge-president-and-ceo-gonzalo-schwarz/ |work=Profectus Magazine |date=January 31, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Rollet |first1=Charles |title=Defense tech startup Epirus raises $250M Series D to counter drone swarms |url=https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/04/defense-tech-startup-epirus-raises-250m-series-d-to-counter-drone-swarms/ |access-date=August 14, 2025 |work=TechCrunch |date=March 5, 2025}}</ref>

The German unicorn [[Helsing (company)|Helsing]] was built as a German or European answer to Palantir.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hellrand |first1=Maris |title=Helsing: the AI powerhouse reshaping Europe´s military future — Trade with Estonia |url=https://tradewithestonia.com/helsing-the-ai-powerhouse-reshaping-europes-military-future/ |access-date=August 14, 2025 |date=June 6, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Warum Spotify-Gründer Daniel Ek jetzt Milliarden in Helsing steckt |url=https://www.investmentweek.com/warum-spotify-grunder-daniel-ek-jetzt-milliarden-in-helsing-steckt/ |access-date=August 14, 2025 |work=InvestmentWeek |date=June 22, 2025 |language=de}}</ref> Thiel also had a role in Helsing's success, and the company is considered part of the "Thiel ecosystem", though he does not directly invest in it.<ref name="espreso">{{cite news |last1=Amelin |first1=Anatoliy |title=Bilderberg Club 2025 and evolution of Ukrainian topic |url=https://global.espreso.tv/world-news-bilderberg-club-evolution-of-ukrainian-topic |access-date=July 31, 2025 |work=global.espreso.tv |date=July 7, 2025 |language=en}}</ref><ref name="arctic">{{cite news |last1=Dromberg |first1=Jonas |title=Why Sweden has the necessary tools to lead new Nordic defense drive |url=https://www.arctictoday.com/why-sweden-has-the-necessary-tools-to-lead-nordic-new-defence-drive/ |access-date=July 31, 2025 |work=ArcticToday |date=April 2, 2025}}</ref> Govradar is a startup focused on public procurement for the German government and Bundeswehr. It was founded in 2020 by former Palantir employee Sascha Goyk, who has also served as a [[Reservist Association of Deutsche Bundeswehr|Bundeswehr reserve officer]] since 2005. His task at Palantir was developing solutions to make the company's data analytics platform accessible to German police and intelligence agencies, but because Palantir faced intense scrutiny, he left and founded his own company.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Mathewson |first1=Fiona |title=Beschaffung neu denken: Soldat baut ein Check24 für den Staat |url=https://www.businessinsider.de/gruenderszene/business/govradar-check24-fuer-den-staat-c/ |work=Business Insider |date=December 19, 2022 |language=de-DE}}</ref> Nordic Air Defence (NAV) is a Swedish startup developing counter-drone missile technology that is staffed by former employees of Palantir and [[Quantum-Systems]], which is backed by [[Thiel Capital]]. Thiel-backed [[Peter Thiel#SNÖ Ventures|SNÖ Ventures]] invests in NAV.<ref name="arctic" />

The French startup Comand AI, founded in 2023 by Antoine Chassang (ex-[[Snapchat]]) and Loïc Mougeolle (ex-[[Naval Group]]), aims to be a more adaptive alternative to Palantir. Its team was poached from Palantir, OpenAI, and Anduril.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sauveton |first1=Pierre |title=IA militaire: l'OTAN choisit Palantir, la France riposte |url=https://opexnews.fr/ia-militaire-otan-palantir-france-artemis-genial-comand-ai/ |access-date=August 14, 2025 |work=OpexNews |date=April 23, 2025 |language=fr-FR}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=La start-up d'IA militaire Comand AI recrute chez le géant Anduril - 28/04/2025 |url=https://www.lalettre.fr/fr/entreprises_defense-et-aeronautique/2025/04/28/la-start-up-d-ia-militaire-comand-ai-recrute-chez-le-geant-anduril,110440670-bre |access-date=August 14, 2025 |work=La Lettre |date=April 28, 2025 |language=fr}}</ref> The British company Arondite was founded by Will Blyth (also CEO), a former British Army officer who had also worked for Palantir and Helsing.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Matthews |first1=Jason |title=AI defence tech startup Arondite secures $12M to connect autonomous systems at scale — TFN |url=https://techfundingnews.com/ai-defence-technology-arondite-raises-12m/ |access-date=August 14, 2025 |work=Tech Funding News |date=May 1, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> The Israeli defense tech startup Kela was founded by Hamutal Meridor (president, former officer in the Israeli army, and former head of Palantir's general operations in Israel), Alon Dror (CEO and former Israeli tank platoon commander), Jason Manne, and Omer Bar Ilan. Like Palantir, Kela develops commercial and military systems "in service of Western defense".<ref>{{cite web |title=Kela |url=https://kelasys.com/ |access-date=October 11, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title="To build Israeli companies like Anduril or Palantir, we must focus on comprehensive solutions, not niche ones" |url=https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/r1nf009x5kg |access-date=August 14, 2025 |work=ctech |date=February 17, 2025 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Kela: The Israeli startup rewriting how wars are fought |url=https://www.calcalistech.com/ctechnews/article/ftop7r3p8 |work=ctech |date=April 30, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> London-based Conduct is a company founded by three ex-Palantir employees to overhaul legacy ERP systems, with a focus on [[SAP]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Escárzaga |first1=Antonio L. |title=London-based Conduct secures €11.2 million to modernise enterprise IT systems with AI |url=https://www.eu-startups.com/2025/09/london-based-conduct-secures-e11-2-million-to-modernise-enterprise-it-systems-with-ai/ |work=EU-Startups |date=September 18, 2025}}</ref>

In 2024, alongside [[General Catalyst]] and Red Cell Partners, the [[Founders Fund]] incubated the defense incubator Valinor Enterprises. The co-founders are former Palantir senior vice president Julie Bush (CEO), [[Trae Stephens]], General Catalyst's Paul Kwan, and Red Cell's Grant Verstandig.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Demarest |first1=Colin |title=Exclusive: Valinor wants to build defense-tech "picks and shovels" |url=https://www.axios.com/2025/10/15/valinor-harbor-bush-stephens-kwan |work=Axios |date=October 15, 2025 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Irvine |first1=Bethany |title=Playbook PM: Senate border talks in limbo |url=https://www.politico.com/newsletters/playbook-pm/2023/12/04/senate-border-talks-in-limbo-00129921 |work=Politico |date=December 4, 2023 |language=en}}</ref> The team is sourced from Palantir, Anduril, and Helsing. The firm also has strategic relationships with these companies.<ref>{{cite web |title=Seeding the Future with Valinor |url=https://www.generalcatalyst.com/stories/seeding-the-future-with-valinor |website=generalcatalyst.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=In conversation with General Catalyst |publisher=Resilience Media |url=https://www.resiliencemedia.co/p/in-conversation-with-general-catalyst |language=en}}</ref> In 2025, biodefense company Valthos emerged from stealth, with backing from [[Lux Capital]], [[OpenAI]], and Founders Fund. Its co-founders are Kathleen McMahon (CEO), who previously served as leader of Palantir's life sciences division, and Lux Capital partner Tess van Stekelenburg.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Chapman |first1=Lizette |title=OpenAI Backs a New Venture Trying to Thwart AI Bio-Attacks |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-10-24/openai-backs-a-new-venture-trying-to-thwart-ai-bio-attacks |date=October 24, 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20251024110939/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-10-24/openai-backs-a-new-venture-trying-to-thwart-ai-bio-attacks |archive-date=October 24, 2025}}</ref> Beijing-based Deepexi (滴普科技) is seen as China's effort to build a version of Palantir. It went public in Hong Kong on October 28, 2025.<ref name="stcn">{{cite news |author1=王智佳 (Wang Zhijia) |title=中国版Palantir滴普科技成功登陆港股，首日开盘股价飙涨超111%，开启价值重估新征程 |url=https://www.stcn.com/article/detail/3427491.html |work=stcn.com |date=October 28, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Four IPOs to be gray market tonight; short-term trading recommended |url=https://www.thestandard.com.hk/wealth-and-investment/article/315194/Four-IPOs-to-be-gray-market-tonight-short-term-trading-recommended |work=The Standard |location=Hong Kong |date=October 27, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> Its website calls it an "enterprise- large-model artificial intelligence (AI) application solution provider and a National Specialised and Innovative 'Little Giant' Enterprise", using a "general-purpose enterprise operational decision-making large model".<ref>{{cite web |title=Deepexi Technology Co., Ltd. |url=https://www.investhk.gov.hk/en/our-clients/deepexi-technology-co-ltd/ |website=Deepexi Technology Co., Ltd. |access-date=November 3, 2025}}</ref> The IPO prompted Brijesh Singh, a senior [[Indian Police Service|IPS officer]], to call on India to build a similar company to treat data as strategic infrastructure and fuse the military and civil spheres.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Parul |first1=Prakriti |title=Beijing Built its Palantir : Where is India's? |url=https://sundayguardianlive.com/editors-choice/beijing-built-its-palantir-where-is-indias-159187/ |work=The Sunday Guardian |date=November 1, 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

===Palantir Startup Fellowship===
Palantir Startup Fellowship is a global program designed to accelerate AI startups and help them to integrate their infrastructure with Palantir's software.<ref>{{cite news |title=Inhance, Palantir Technologies 'Startup Fellowship' Finalist |url=https://www.venturesquare.net/en/977088 |publisher=벤처스퀘어 |date=May 26, 2025 |language=ko-KR}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Palantir for Builders {{!}} Startups |url=https://www.palantir.com/offerings/palantir-for-builders/startups/ |publisher=Palantir |access-date=November 15, 2025 |language=en}}</ref> Companies selected for the first cohort include Korean Enhans (solutions for real-time decision making),<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hojun |first1=Jeong |title=Advance, which develops artificial intelligence (AI) technology specializing in commerce, announced .. - MK |url=https://www.mk.co.kr/en/it/11363525 |publisher=매일경제 |date=July 9, 2025 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=How Enhans Strengthens Its AI Automation Solution with Support from Palantir - Technology Org |url=https://www.technology.org/2025/06/10/how-enhans-strengthens-its-ai-automation-solution-with-support-from-palantir/ |date=June 10, 2025}}</ref> and Rune Technologies (a military logistics company founded by Anduril alumni and backed by [[Andreessen Horowitz]]).<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wilkers |first1=Ross |title=Rune Technologies fetches $24M in Series A capital |url=https://www.washingtontechnology.com/companies/2025/07/rune-technologies-fetches-24m-series-capital/406930/ |work=Washington Technology |date=July 23, 2025 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Chowdhry |first1=Amit |title=Rune Technologies: $24 Million Series A Raised For Military Logistics Software |url=https://pulse2.com/rune-technologies-24-million-series-a-raised-for-military-logistics-software/ |work=Pulse 2.0 |date=July 22, 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

